        {
            "id": "25485496",
            "user": "Upnorth Here",
            "timestamp": "18:37, 25 October 2018",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(29,640 bytes)",
            "change": 497,
            "summary": "(→‎Steps:  add details to cable types and where certain types are prohibited)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Understand non-metallic cables. NM (also called \"Romex®\") and UF (\"underground feeder\") cables both have plastic jacket wrapped around 2 (or more) insulated wires - including one white and one black - and a single uninsulated wire.<ref>http://www.homedepot.com/c/factors_to_consider_when_wiring_your_home_HT_BG_EL</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 33.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 33-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 33.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 33.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 33.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#* NM cable is easier to work than other cable types, requires no special tools or preparation, and costs less. For these reasons, it is widely used. It is not, however, authorized for use in all installations, such as being embedded in concrete or where exposed to the risk of physical damage.",
                    "#Understand armored cable types, including BX, MC and AC. Armored cable types are very similar with only slight variations. They consist of a metal jacket formed by an interlocking, helically wound band of steel or aluminum around two (or more) insulated wires - including one white, one black, and often one green. Cables lacking a green insulated wire use the outer metal jacket as the ground conductor. Type AC is fabricated with a continuous internal bonding conductor in contact with the metal sheath.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 34.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 34-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 34.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 34.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 34.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#* Of these armored cable types, only Type NM can be installed outside or for direct burial underground, and only if listed (tested) and labeled for that purpose.",
                    "#Know the restrictions of each cable type. There are unique precautions and instructions for each type and special connectors for armored cables. For example, do not use a Romex (Type NM) connector on an armored cable, even though many connectors appear similar.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 35.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 35-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 35.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 35.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 35.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Become familiar with cable naming conventions. All of these cables have \"trade names\" which are basically derived from the number of insulated non-ground conductors, construction type and manufacturers' names. For example, a \"twelve-two (12/2) Romex®\" cable has two #12 conductors, plus a full sized #12 ground. A \"fourteen-three (14/3) BX\" cable is a metal-armored cable with three #14 conductors, plus a full sized #14 ground. Some types of BX are also tested and approved for using the sheath as a grounding or bonding conductor.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 37.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 37-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 37.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 37.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 37.360p.first.gif}}"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Understand non-metallic cables. NM (also called \"Romex\") and UF (\"underground feeder\") cables both have plastic jacket wrapped around 2 (or more) insulated wires - including one white and one black - and a single uninsulated wire.<ref>http://www.homedepot.com/c/factors_to_consider_when_wiring_your_home_HT_BG_EL</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 33.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 33-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 33.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 33.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 33.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#* NM cable is easier to work than other cable types, requires no special tools or preparation, and costs less. For these reasons, it is widely used.",
                    "#Understand armored cable types, including BX, MC and AC. Armored cable types are very similar with only slight variations. They consist of a metal jacket formed by an interlocking, helically wound band of steel or aluminum around two (or more) insulated wires - including one white, one black, and often one green. Cables lacking a green insulated wire use the outer metal jacket as the ground conductor.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 34.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 34-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 34.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 34.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 34.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#* None of these armored cable types can be installed outside or underground.",
                    "#Know the restrictions of each cable type. There are unique precautions and instructions for each type and special connectors for armored cables. For example, do not use a Romex connector on an armored cable, even though many connectors appear similar.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 35.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 35-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 35.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 35.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 35.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Become familiar with cable naming conventions. All of these cables have \"trade names\" which are basically derived from the number of insulated non-ground conductors and construction type. For example, a \"twelve two (12/2) Romex\" cable has two #12 conductors, plus a full sized #12 ground. A \"fourteen three (14/3) BX\" cable has three #14 conductors, plus a full sized #14 ground.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 37.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 37-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 37.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 37.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 37.360p.first.gif}}"
                ]
            }
        },
