            "summary": "(reordering tips and warnings based on votes)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "adjustment",
            "reverted_by": "Anna",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "*Never install a gauge wire on a circuit that is protected by a breaker or fuse that has a higher capacity than the wire you're using: 6 gauge - 50A, 8 gauge - 40A, 10 gauge - 30A, 12 gauge - 20A, 14 gauge - 15A.  Do not connect smaller wires to the electric panel, unless they are the leads of a transformer for a doorbell or similar circuit.",
                    "*Use the smaller, easier to work and less expensive #14 (14 gauge) copper Romex cable if the circuit is protected by a 15 amp fuse or circuit breaker.  Very few three-way circuits switch loads on a 20 amp circuit."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "*Never install a gauge wire on a circuit that is protected by a breaker or fuse that has a higher capacity than the wire you're using: 6 gauge - 50A, 8 gauge - 40A, 10 gauge - 30A, 12 gauge - 20A, 14 gauge - 15A.  Do not connect smaller wires to the electric panel, unless they are the leads of a transformer for a doorbell or similar circuit.",
                    "*Use the smaller, easier to work and less expensive #14 (14 gauge) copper Romex cable if the circuit is protected by a 15 amp fuse or circuit breaker.  Very few three-way circuits switch loads on a 20 amp circuit."
                ]
            }
        },
