        {
            "id": "20095851",
            "user": "Wikivisual",
            "timestamp": "19:07, 25 April 2016",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(28,932 bytes)",
            "change": 8339,
            "summary": "(Saving new step-by-step photos)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "major_addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Flip off power in the room you're working. Head to your fuse box and make sure there is no power coming to the room you're currently working in. Use a voltage detector to ensure that you're not going to shock yourself once you start working.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 1.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 1-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 1 Version 3.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 1.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 1.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Screw your two boxes and light fixture into place, then gather your supplies. Before you even start wiring, you want to get the fixtures securely in place so that you can effectively wire everything together. You will need:<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 2.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 2-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 2 Version 3.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Slide the 2-wire NM power cable into the first switch box. Take your power wire, coming from the house, and slide it into the first switch box. Choose a direction that will make it easy for you to then thread the wire towards your second switch box.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 3.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 3-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 3 Version 3.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 3.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 3.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Remove about 9 inches of sheathing from the wire. Strip off enough of the rubber coating around your NM wire to expose the wires underneath. Some people prefer to do this before feeding into the switch box, but it doesn't matter.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 4.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 4-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 4 Version 3.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Run a 3-wire NM cable between the first and second boxes. Strip 6-9\" on one end to expose the three separate wires, then run the first half into your first switch box. Repeat on the other end and run it into your second switch box. Cut the cable to length if there is extra once you pull through the second box.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 5.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 5-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 5 Version 3.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 5.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 5.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Use a cable staple to secure the wires to the wall or stud within 8\" of the box. Cables must be secured so that, if there is tugging or pulling further down the line, the live wires don't rip out of the box and cause issues. Some boxes have clamps to hold onto the wires. Current electrical codes require any switch box without built-in clamps to have the wires secured within eight inches of the box.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 6.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 6-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 6 Version 3.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 6.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 6.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Pull another 2-wire NM cable through the second switch box, just like you did in the very beginning with the first box. The only difference is that this 2-wire cable is '''not''' a power cable from the wall -- it is a free-hanging cable that you will use to attach the light. Be sure to strip 9\" and pull the wires through into the second switch box.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 7.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 7-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 7 Version 3.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 7.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 7.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Pull the 2-wire cable from the second box up and into the light fixture. Strip away 6-9\" of the sheathing and pull your wires in so that they can attach to the light. Again, be sure to use a cable staple to support the wire within 8\" of the box.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 8.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 8-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 8 Version 3.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 8.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 8.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Strip 1\" off of the individual ends of all the smaller wires. A 2-wire NM cable is actually made up of smaller wires, run together for convenience. You exposed those wires already, but now you need to strip the rubber sheath off of the very end of each of these little wires.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 9.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 9-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 9 Version 3.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 9.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 9.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Curve the ends of every black and red cable into small hooks. This will make them easier to attach to the actual switches. You'll hook this around a screw, which you then tighten to clamp down the wire to the light switch.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 10.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 10-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 10 Version 3.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 10.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 10.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Use wire nuts to attach the each set of two white wires together. Twist the two white, neutral wires together in each box and twist on a wire nut. Keep twisting until the wires are secured and the nut stays in place when let go. There are two sets to attach--<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 11.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 11-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 11 Version 3.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 11.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 11.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Use wire nuts to attach the two pairs of green/orange grounding wires together, adding a 6\" piece of copper grounding wire to each pair as well. Take all three ends and hold them together, then twist on the wire nut so they are all securely fastened together. You should have two wires tracing back through your NM cables, and one hanging ground wire on each set.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 12.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 12-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 12 Version 3.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 12.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 12.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#After making all your connections, fold the wires neatly into the box. You want to zig-zag them, so they come about a bit like an accordion. You can tuck the wires in the light switch back too -- though they didn't get any work done. Your two switch boxes should each be wired identically, like so:<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 13.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 13-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 13 Version 3.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 13.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 13.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Replace the drywall, coverings, etc. '''before moving on.''' By now, the wiring part is actually done. You'll just need to attach the cables appropriately to the switches and light now -- but the fixtures will be uncovered by your wall anyway. Everything \"behind the scenes\" is mostly done.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 14.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 14-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 14 Version 2.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 14.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 14.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Attach the black power cable (from the wall) to the general screw (black) in the three-way light switch. Hook the curled end of the wire under the screw, then tighten down the screw to hold the wire firmly in place. This screw is usually in the upper left side of the switch and is painted black, but check the guide that comes with your switch if you're unsure.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 15.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 15-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 15 Version 2.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 15.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 15.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Attach the remaining black and red cables (from 3-wire NM) to the traveler screws on the switch. These screws, often gold, will be on opposite sides of the switch. Hook the red one up to the side with you black power wire, and the black on the identical screw on the other side of the switch.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 16.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 16-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 16 Version 2.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 16.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 16.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Attach the hanging ground wire (the pigtail) to the green grounding screw. There should only be one screw left, and it is for that small, 6\" free cable attached to the grounding wires. Loop it around the screw and fasten it down like any other cable.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 17.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 17-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 17 Version 2.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 17.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 17.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Attach the black wire leading to the light to your general screw (black). The wiring on the second switch is nearly identical to the first, except for this key difference: '''the black wire goes to the light, not to the power source.\" Once you have this wire screwed down, the rest are identical:<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 18.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 18-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 18 Version 2.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 18.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 18.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Attach the appropriate wires to your light fixture. You should have a space for a neutral wire (white), a grounding wire, (orange or green) and a power wire (black). They will screw on easily, and your light is set. Replace the covers for the switches, screw them into place, and turn the power back on to be in business.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 19.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 19-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 19 Version 2.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 19.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 19.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Select the proper wire size. If originating from an electric panel or fuse box, #12 copper is the minimum size when connecting to a 20 amp circuit breaker or fuse; #14 copper is the minimum size when connecting to a 15 amp circuit breaker or fuse (aluminum wire on circuits of these capacities has been prohibited for many years).<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 20.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 20-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 20.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 20.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 20.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Select the proper cable type.  The power supply or feed cable should be \"2 wire\" (or conductor) cable ''plus'' a ground wire. See below for descriptions and uses of common cable types.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 21.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 21-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 21.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 21.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 21.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Shut power off.  This is a very important step.  Please do not skip it.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 22.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 22-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 22.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 22.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 22.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Install a 2 wire cable between the power source (outlet box, electric panel, etc) and the first switch box.  Leave {{convert|8|-|10|in|cm|1}} of wire inside both boxes (source and first switch) before cutting the cable to facilitate easy connection to the switch and power source.  Connect the ground wire to the circuit ground wire(s) with a wire nut or other approved connection (see How to [[Connect Electrical Wire]]). The ground wire should be connected to the neutral terminal bar. Finally, connect the black wire to the hot supply or the circuit breaker / fuse and the white to the neutral supply or neutral terminal bar in the electrical panel.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 23.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 23-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 23.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 23.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 23.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Install a 3 wire cable from the first switch box to the light fixture's box.  Leave {{convert|8|-|10|in|cm|1}} of wire inside each of the boxes before cutting the cable to facilitate easy splicing and connection to the switch and fixture.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 24.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 24-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 24.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 24.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 24.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Install a 3 wire cable from the second switch box to the light fixture's box.  Leave {{convert|8|-|10|in|cm|1}} of wire inside each box before cutting the cable to facilitate easy splicing and connection to the fixture.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 25.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 25-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 25.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 25.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 25.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Connect ground wires.  Provide as shorter lengths (8 inches/20.5 cm) of bare or green insulated wire to this wire-nutted group to allow connection to green ground terminal screws on each device in the box (switches, outlets, fixtures, etc.) - one length of insulated wire per ground terminal screw. If the switch or junction box is metal, it too ''must be securely grounded'' with a green ground screw or approved grounding clip.  This must be done at each and every box that a cable enters and at each device that provides a termination point for ground.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 26.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 26-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 26.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 26.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 26.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Connect feed wires in the first switch box.  First, connect all ground wires as previously described.  The 2 wire cable feed from the power source enters the bottom of the switch box and the hot (black) wire connects to the common or shunt terminal on the 3-way switch.  There is only one such terminal on a 3-way switch, and it is usually identified as the one having a different color terminal screw (often significantly darker) from the other two terminal screws (not counting the green ground screw).<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 27.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 27-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 27.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 27.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 27.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Connect 3 wire cable in the first switch box.  The 3 wire cable enters through the top of the first switch box. The red insulated wire connects to either one of the 2 unused terminal screws (shown in the image above to be on the top left and right sides of both 3-way switches).  It does not matter to which terminal screw this wire is connected.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 28.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 28-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 28.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 28.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 28.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Connect wires in the light fixture's box.  Again, connect all ground wires as previously described, if not already completed.  In the light fixture's box, there will be two 3 wire cables. One 3 wire cable originates at the first switch box and has its white insulated wire as a neutral. The other 3 wire cable originates at the second switch box and will become the \"switch leg\". Mark both ends of this wire by wrapping it with black electrical tape to alert others working on the circuit later that it is no longer neutral. This is a newer electrical code requirement, but has been a common practice whenever a white or gray wire is / or can become hot.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 29.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 29-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 29.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 29.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 29.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Connect 3 wire cable in the second switch box to the switch.  Connect all ground wires as previously described, if not already completed. Connect the black insulated wire to the shunt or common terminal screw of the switch (once again, the common screw terminal is the different colored screw from the rest of those on the switch).<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 30.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 30-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 30.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 30.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 30.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Connect the fixture.  The light fixture's box should have only a black wire, a white wire and a ground wire to supply power to the fixture.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 31.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 31-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 31.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 31.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 31.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Finish up.  Check all wire nuts for tightness and exposed neutral and hot conductor surfaces. Fold all wires carefully into the boxes and secure devices and fixture with screws.  Install plates and covers.  Restore power and test.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 32.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 32-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 32.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 32.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 32.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Understand non-metallic cables. NM (also called \"Romex\") and UF (\"underground feeder\") cables both have plastic jacket wrapped around 2 (or more) insulated wires - including one white and one black - and a single uninsulated wire.<ref>http://www.homedepot.com/c/factors_to_consider_when_wiring_your_home_HT_BG_EL</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 33.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 33-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 33.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 33.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 33.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Understand armored cable types, including BX, MC and AC. Armored cable types are very similar with only slight variations. They consist of a metal jacket formed by an interlocking, helically wound band of steel or aluminum around two (or more) insulated wires - including one white, one black, and often one green. Cables lacking a green insulated wire use the outer metal jacket as the ground conductor.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 34.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 34-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 34.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 34.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 34.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Know the restrictions of each cable type. There are unique precautions and instructions for each type and special connectors for armored cables. For example, do not use a Romex connector on an armored cable, even though many connectors appear similar.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 35.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 35-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 35.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 35.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 35.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#If the power source is from an armored cable that lacks a full size (#12 or #14) ground wire, use a metal box to extend the ground from the armored shell to the box and to the circuit's ground wire. Do this by threading a special green, hex-headed grounding machine screw into a pre-tapped hole in the metal box, or use a special green ground clip.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 36.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 36-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 36.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 36.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 36.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Become familiar with cable naming conventions. All of these cables have \"trade names\" which are basically derived from the number of insulated non-ground conductors and construction type. For example, a \"twelve two (12/2) Romex\" cable has two #12 conductors, plus a full sized #12 ground. A \"fourteen three (14/3) BX\" cable has three #14 conductors, plus a full sized #14 ground.<br><br>{{whvid|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 37.360p.mp4|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 37-preview.jpg|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 37.jpg|gif=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 37.360p.gif|giffirst=Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 37.360p.first.gif}}"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Flip off power in the room you're working. Head to your fuse box and make sure there is no power coming to the room you're currently working in. Use a voltage detector to ensure that you're not going to shock yourself once you start working.",
                    "#Screw your two boxes and light fixture into place, then gather your supplies. Before you even start wiring, you want to get the fixtures securely in place so that you can effectively wire everything together. You will need:",
                    "#Slide the 2-wire NM power cable into the first switch box. Take your power wire, coming from the house, and slide it into the first switch box. Choose a direction that will make it easy for you to then thread the wire towards your second switch box.",
                    "#Remove about 9 inches of sheathing from the wire. Strip off enough of the rubber coating around your NM wire to expose the wires underneath. Some people prefer to do this before feeding into the switch box, but it doesn't matter.",
                    "#Run a 3-wire NM cable between the first and second boxes. Strip 6-9\" on one end to expose the three separate wires, then run the first half into your first switch box. Repeat on the other end and run it into your second switch box. Cut the cable to length if there is extra once you pull through the second box.",
                    "#Use a cable staple to secure the wires to the wall or stud within 8\" of the box. Cables must be secured so that, if there is tugging or pulling further down the line, the live wires don't rip out of the box and cause issues. Some boxes have clamps to hold onto the wires. Current electrical codes require any switch box without built-in clamps to have the wires secured within eight inches of the box.",
                    "#Pull another 2-wire NM cable through the second switch box, just like you did in the very beginning with the first box. The only difference is that this 2-wire cable is '''not''' a power cable from the wall -- it is a free-hanging cable that you will use to attach the light. Be sure to strip 9\" and pull the wires through into the second switch box.",
                    "#Pull the 2-wire cable from the second box up and into the light fixture. Strip away 6-9\" of the sheathing and pull your wires in so that they can attach to the light. Again, be sure to use a cable staple to support the wire within 8\" of the box.",
                    "#Strip 1\" off of the individual ends of all the smaller wires. A 2-wire NM cable is actually made up of smaller wires, run together for convenience. You exposed those wires already, but now you need to strip the rubber sheath off of the very end of each of these little wires.",
                    "#Curve the ends of every black and red cable into small hooks. This will make them easier to attach to the actual switches. You'll hook this around a screw, which you then tighten to clamp down the wire to the light switch.",
                    "#Use wire nuts to attach the each set of two white wires together. Twist the two white, neutral wires together in each box and twist on a wire nut. Keep twisting until the wires are secured and the nut stays in place when let go. There are two sets to attach--",
                    "#Use wire nuts to attach the two pairs of green/orange grounding wires together, adding a 6\" piece of copper grounding wire to each pair as well. Take all three ends and hold them together, then twist on the wire nut so they are all securely fastened together. You should have two wires tracing back through your NM cables, and one hanging ground wire on each set.",
                    "#After making all your connections, fold the wires neatly into the box. You want to zig-zag them, so they come about a bit like an accordion. You can tuck the wires in the light switch back too -- though they didn't get any work done. Your two switch boxes should each be wired identically, like so:",
                    "#Replace the drywall, coverings, etc. '''before moving on.''' By now, the wiring part is actually done. You'll just need to attach the cables appropriately to the switches and light now -- but the fixtures will be uncovered by your wall anyway. Everything \"behind the scenes\" is mostly done.",
                    "#Attach the black power cable (from the wall) to the general screw (black) in the three-way light switch. Hook the curled end of the wire under the screw, then tighten down the screw to hold the wire firmly in place. This screw is usually in the upper left side of the switch and is painted black, but check the guide that comes with your switch if you're unsure.",
                    "#Attach the remaining black and red cables (from 3-wire NM) to the traveler screws on the switch. These screws, often gold, will be on opposite sides of the switch. Hook the red one up to the side with you black power wire, and the black on the identical screw on the other side of the switch.",
                    "#Attach the hanging ground wire (the pigtail) to the green grounding screw. There should only be one screw left, and it is for that small, 6\" free cable attached to the grounding wires. Loop it around the screw and fasten it down like any other cable.",
                    "#Attach the black wire leading to the light to your general screw (black). The wiring on the second switch is nearly identical to the first, except for this key difference: '''the black wire goes to the light, not to the power source.\" Once you have this wire screwed down, the rest are identical:",
                    "#Attach the appropriate wires to your light fixture. You should have a space for a neutral wire (white), a grounding wire, (orange or green) and a power wire (black). They will screw on easily, and your light is set. Replace the covers for the switches, screw them into place, and turn the power back on to be in business.",
                    "#Select the proper wire size. If originating from an electric panel or fuse box, #12 copper is the minimum size when connecting to a 20 amp circuit breaker or fuse; #14 copper is the minimum size when connecting to a 15 amp circuit breaker or fuse (aluminum wire on circuits of these capacities has been prohibited for many years).  [[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Select the proper cable type.  The power supply or feed cable should be \"2 wire\" (or conductor) cable ''plus'' a ground wire. See below for descriptions and uses of common cable types. [[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Shut power off.  This is a very important step.  Please do not skip it.[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Install a 2 wire cable between the power source (outlet box, electric panel, etc) and the first switch box.  Leave {{convert|8|-|10|in|cm|1}} of wire inside both boxes (source and first switch) before cutting the cable to facilitate easy connection to the switch and power source.  Connect the ground wire to the circuit ground wire(s) with a wire nut or other approved connection (see How to [[Connect Electrical Wire]]). The ground wire should be connected to the neutral terminal bar. Finally, connect the black wire to the hot supply or the circuit breaker / fuse and the white to the neutral supply or neutral terminal bar in the electrical panel.[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Install a 3 wire cable from the first switch box to the light fixture's box.  Leave {{convert|8|-|10|in|cm|1}} of wire inside each of the boxes before cutting the cable to facilitate easy splicing and connection to the switch and fixture. [[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Install a 3 wire cable from the second switch box to the light fixture's box.  Leave {{convert|8|-|10|in|cm|1}} of wire inside each box before cutting the cable to facilitate easy splicing and connection to the fixture.[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Connect ground wires.  Provide as shorter lengths (8 inches/20.5 cm) of bare or green insulated wire to this wire-nutted group to allow connection to green ground terminal screws on each device in the box (switches, outlets, fixtures, etc.) - one length of insulated wire per ground terminal screw. If the switch or junction box is metal, it too ''must be securely grounded'' with a green ground screw or approved grounding clip.  This must be done at each and every box that a cable enters and at each device that provides a termination point for ground. [[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 7 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Connect feed wires in the first switch box.  First, connect all ground wires as previously described.  The 2 wire cable feed from the power source enters the bottom of the switch box and the hot (black) wire connects to the common or shunt terminal on the 3-way switch.  There is only one such terminal on a 3-way switch, and it is usually identified as the one having a different color terminal screw (often significantly darker) from the other two terminal screws (not counting the green ground screw). [[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 8 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Connect 3 wire cable in the first switch box.  The 3 wire cable enters through the top of the first switch box. The red insulated wire connects to either one of the 2 unused terminal screws (shown in the image above to be on the top left and right sides of both 3-way switches).  It does not matter to which terminal screw this wire is connected.[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 9 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Connect wires in the light fixture's box.  Again, connect all ground wires as previously described, if not already completed.  In the light fixture's box, there will be two 3 wire cables. One 3 wire cable originates at the first switch box and has its white insulated wire as a neutral. The other 3 wire cable originates at the second switch box and will become the \"switch leg\". Mark both ends of this wire by wrapping it with black electrical tape to alert others working on the circuit later that it is no longer neutral. This is a newer electrical code requirement, but has been a common practice whenever a white or gray wire is / or can become hot. [[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 10 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Connect 3 wire cable in the second switch box to the switch.  Connect all ground wires as previously described, if not already completed. Connect the black insulated wire to the shunt or common terminal screw of the switch (once again, the common screw terminal is the different colored screw from the rest of those on the switch).[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 11 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Connect the fixture.  The light fixture's box should have only a black wire, a white wire and a ground wire to supply power to the fixture.[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 12 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Finish up.  Check all wire nuts for tightness and exposed neutral and hot conductor surfaces. Fold all wires carefully into the boxes and secure devices and fixture with screws.  Install plates and covers.  Restore power and test.[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 13 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Understand non-metallic cables. NM (also called \"Romex\") and UF (\"underground feeder\") cables both have plastic jacket wrapped around 2 (or more) insulated wires - including one white and one black - and a single uninsulated wire.<ref>http://www.homedepot.com/c/factors_to_consider_when_wiring_your_home_HT_BG_EL</ref> [[Image:40459 20.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Understand armored cable types, including BX, MC and AC. Armored cable types are very similar with only slight variations. They consist of a metal jacket formed by an interlocking, helically wound band of steel or aluminum around two (or more) insulated wires - including one white, one black, and often one green. Cables lacking a green insulated wire use the outer metal jacket as the ground conductor. [[Image:40459 21.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "# Know the restrictions of each cable type. There are unique precautions and instructions for each type and special connectors for armored cables. For example, do not use a Romex connector on an armored cable, even though many connectors appear similar. [[Image:40459 22.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "# If the power source is from an armored cable that lacks a full size (#12 or #14) ground wire, use a metal box to extend the ground from the armored shell to the box and to the circuit's ground wire. Do this by threading a special green, hex-headed grounding machine screw into a pre-tapped hole in the metal box, or use a special green ground clip. [[Image:40459 23.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "# Become familiar with cable naming conventions. All of these cables have \"trade names\" which are basically derived from the number of insulated non-ground conductors and construction type. For example, a \"twelve two (12/2) Romex\" cable has two #12 conductors, plus a full sized #12 ground. A \"fourteen three (14/3) BX\" cable has three #14 conductors, plus a full sized #14 ground. [[Image:40459 24.jpg|center|416px]]"
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "20076862",
            "user": "Ngeisler",
            "timestamp": "20:41, 22 April 2016",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(20,593 bytes)",
            "change": 5711,
            "summary": "(edits for accuracy/helpfulness, prepping for video)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "major_addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "A 3-way light switch allows you to control a light source from two separate points.<ref>http://www.homeimprovementweb.com/information/how-to/three-way-switch.htm</ref> Although a 3 way is one of the hardest electrical circuits to figure out, it's also one of the most useful.",
                    "",
                    "'''Note:''' there are several ways to install a 3 Way Light Switch. The methods shown here are some of the safest and most versatile, though not the only options.",
                    "===Wiring the Switches===",
                    "#Flip off power in the room you're working. Head to your fuse box and make sure there is no power coming to the room you're currently working in. Use a voltage detector to ensure that you're not going to shock yourself once you start working.",
                    "#Screw your two boxes and light fixture into place, then gather your supplies. Before you even start wiring, you want to get the fixtures securely in place so that you can effectively wire everything together. You will need:",
                    "#*Two 3-Way Switches",
                    "#*Two 2-wire non-metallic (NM) cable",
                    "#*One 3-wire NM cables",
                    "#*2 6\" pieces of grounding wire",
                    "#Slide the 2-wire NM power cable into the first switch box. Take your power wire, coming from the house, and slide it into the first switch box. Choose a direction that will make it easy for you to then thread the wire towards your second switch box.",
                    "#Remove about 9 inches of sheathing from the wire. Strip off enough of the rubber coating around your NM wire to expose the wires underneath. Some people prefer to do this before feeding into the switch box, but it doesn't matter.",
                    "#*You want roughly 3\" of wire in the box, and 6\" outside the box.",
                    "#Run a 3-wire NM cable between the first and second boxes. Strip 6-9\" on one end to expose the three separate wires, then run the first half into your first switch box. Repeat on the other end and run it into your second switch box. Cut the cable to length if there is extra once you pull through the second box.",
                    "#*This third wire allows power to run to your second switch even if the first one is off.",
                    "#Use a cable staple to secure the wires to the wall or stud within 8\" of the box. Cables must be secured so that, if there is tugging or pulling further down the line, the live wires don't rip out of the box and cause issues. Some boxes have clamps to hold onto the wires. Current electrical codes require any switch box without built-in clamps to have the wires secured within eight inches of the box.",
                    "#*Secure to the center of the stud, in the wall, so that it will not be hit by drywall studs later on if you were to hang anything near the light.",
                    "#Pull another 2-wire NM cable through the second switch box, just like you did in the very beginning with the first box. The only difference is that this 2-wire cable is '''not''' a power cable from the wall -- it is a free-hanging cable that you will use to attach the light. Be sure to strip 9\" and pull the wires through into the second switch box.",
                    "#*Be sure to secure all wires within 8\" of the box.",
                    "#Pull the 2-wire cable from the second box up and into the light fixture. Strip away 6-9\" of the sheathing and pull your wires in so that they can attach to the light. Again, be sure to use a cable staple to support the wire within 8\" of the box.",
                    "#Strip 1\" off of the individual ends of all the smaller wires. A 2-wire NM cable is actually made up of smaller wires, run together for convenience. You exposed those wires already, but now you need to strip the rubber sheath off of the very end of each of these little wires.",
                    "#Curve the ends of every black and red cable into small hooks. This will make them easier to attach to the actual switches. You'll hook this around a screw, which you then tighten to clamp down the wire to the light switch.",
                    "#*The should look like little canes.",
                    "#*This is often easiest with needle-nose pliers.",
                    "#Use wire nuts to attach the each set of two white wires together. Twist the two white, neutral wires together in each box and twist on a wire nut. Keep twisting until the wires are secured and the nut stays in place when let go. There are two sets to attach--",
                    "#*The pair in the first switch box",
                    "#*The pair in the second switch box",
                    "#Use wire nuts to attach the two pairs of green/orange grounding wires together, adding a 6\" piece of copper grounding wire to each pair as well. Take all three ends and hold them together, then twist on the wire nut so they are all securely fastened together. You should have two wires tracing back through your NM cables, and one hanging ground wire on each set.",
                    "#*This extra grounding wire will be attached to the light switches. It is called a \"pigtail\" in electrical parlance.",
                    "#After making all your connections, fold the wires neatly into the box. You want to zig-zag them, so they come about a bit like an accordion. You can tuck the wires in the light switch back too -- though they didn't get any work done. Your two switch boxes should each be wired identically, like so:",
                    "#*Two white, neutral wires tied off by a wire nut.",
                    "#*Two grounding wires, plus one pigtail, tied off by a wire nut.",
                    "#*A red wire, with the end stripped and curled.",
                    "#*A black wire, with the end stripped and curled.",
                    "#Replace the drywall, coverings, etc. '''before moving on.''' By now, the wiring part is actually done. You'll just need to attach the cables appropriately to the switches and light now -- but the fixtures will be uncovered by your wall anyway. Everything \"behind the scenes\" is mostly done.",
                    "",
                    "===Installing the Switches and Light===",
                    "#Attach the black power cable (from the wall) to the general screw (black) in the three-way light switch. Hook the curled end of the wire under the screw, then tighten down the screw to hold the wire firmly in place. This screw is usually in the upper left side of the switch and is painted black, but check the guide that comes with your switch if you're unsure.",
                    "#*Not all switches are made for three-way wiring. Make sure you purchase accordingly.",
                    "#*There should be two black wires in your box -- one from the power source, one leading to the other box. Make sure you use the power wire.",
                    "#Attach the remaining black and red cables (from 3-wire NM) to the traveler screws on the switch. These screws, often gold, will be on opposite sides of the switch. Hook the red one up to the side with you black power wire, and the black on the identical screw on the other side of the switch.",
                    "#Attach the hanging ground wire (the pigtail) to the green grounding screw. There should only be one screw left, and it is for that small, 6\" free cable attached to the grounding wires. Loop it around the screw and fasten it down like any other cable.",
                    "#Attach the black wire leading to the light to your general screw (black). The wiring on the second switch is nearly identical to the first, except for this key difference: '''the black wire goes to the light, not to the power source.\" Once you have this wire screwed down, the rest are identical:",
                    "#*Attach the grouped red and black cables (from 3-wire NM) to the traveler screws",
                    "#*Attach the pigtail wire to the grounding screw.",
                    "#Attach the appropriate wires to your light fixture. You should have a space for a neutral wire (white), a grounding wire, (orange or green) and a power wire (black). They will screw on easily, and your light is set. Replace the covers for the switches, screw them into place, and turn the power back on to be in business.",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "=== Alternative Wirings ===",
                    "#* The size of all the wires in any circuit ''must'' be the same size.  When obtaining power from a nearby electrical outlet or another circuit device, the new wiring must be the same size as those that supply the outlet.",
                    "#Install a 2 wire cable between the power source (outlet box, electric panel, etc) and the first switch box.  Leave {{convert|8|-|10|in|cm|1}} of wire inside both boxes (source and first switch) before cutting the cable to facilitate easy connection to the switch and power source.  Connect the ground wire to the circuit ground wire(s) with a wire nut or other approved connection (see How to [[Connect Electrical Wire]]). The ground wire should be connected to the neutral terminal bar. Finally, connect the black wire to the hot supply or the circuit breaker / fuse and the white to the neutral supply or neutral terminal bar in the electrical panel.[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* No ground connection is made to plastic, fiber or another non-conductive box.",
                    "# Know the restrictions of each cable type. There are unique precautions and instructions for each type and special connectors for armored cables. For example, do not use a Romex connector on an armored cable, even though many connectors appear similar. [[Image:40459 22.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "*Use the smaller, easier to work and less expensive #14 (14 gauge) copper Romex cable if the circuit is protected by a 15 amp fuse or circuit breaker.  Very few three-way circuits switch loads on a 20 amp circuit.",
                    "*One wire per terminal.  Do not connect more than one wire under a screw terminal. Additionally, the wire should wrap clockwise around the screw.  Only wrap solid wire around a terminal screw.  Stranded wire will require fork or ring terminals to be installed (crimped or soldered) on the wire and the terminal screw tightened down on the fork or ring terminal."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "A 3 way light switch allows you to control a light source from two separate points.<ref>http://www.homeimprovementweb.com/information/how-to/three-way-switch.htm</ref> Although a 3 way is one of the hardest electrical circuits to figure out, it's also one of the most useful. See Step 1 for  one of the easiest ways to do 3 way light switch wiring.",
                    "=== Wiring a 3 Way Switch ===",
                    "#* The size of all the wires in any circuit ''must'' be the same size.  When obtaining power from a nearby electrical outlet or other circuit device, the new wiring must be the same size as those that supply the outlet.",
                    "#Install a 2 wire cable between the power source (outlet box, electric panel, etc) and the first switch box.  Leave {{convert|8|-|10|in|cm|1}} of wire inside both boxes (source and first switch) before cutting the cable to facilitate easy connection to the switch and power source.  Connect the ground wire to the circuit ground wire(s) with a wirenut or other approved connection (see How to [[Connect Electrical Wire]]). The ground wire should be connected to the neutral terminal bar. Finally, connect the black wire to the hot supply or the circuit breaker / fuse and the white to the neutral supply or neutral terminal bar in the electrical panel.[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* No ground connection is made to plastic, fiber or other non-conductive box.",
                    "",
                    "=== Instructions for Installing in Austria ===",
                    "# Disconnect circuit power. Make sure there is no power on the circuit.[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 14.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Connect circuit cables to the light. The circuit's Earth (green) and Neutral (black) cables connect to the light's green & blue cables, respectively.[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 15.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Connect the circuit's active (red) wire to switch ONE common terminal (middle). Connect the switching wire (white) to Terminal 1. Connect the second switching wire (white or red) to Terminal 2.[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 16.jpg|center]]",
                    "# On the switch, connect both switching wires (terminal 1 and terminal 2 respectively on switch 2) and the common terminal to the red wire. This then gets connected to the light.[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 17.jpg|center]]",
                    "# At the light fitting, connect switch one's switching wire 1 to switch two's switching wire 1. Then connect switch one's switching wire 2 to switch two's switching wire 2.[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 18.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Connect switch 2's red wire (connected to switch 2's common terminal) to the active terminal on the light fitting (red or brown).[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 19.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Know the restrictions of each cable type. There are unique precautions and instructions for each type, and special connectors for armored cables. For example, do not use a Romex connector on an armored cable, even though many connectors appear similar. [[Image:40459 22.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "",
                    "*Use the smaller, easier to work and less expensive #14 (14 gauge) copper Romex cable if the circuit is protected by a 15 amp fuse or circuit breaker.  Very few three way circuits switch loads on a 20 amp circuit.",
                    "*One wire per terminal.  Do not connect more than one wire under a screw terminal. Additionally, wire should wrap clockwise around the screw.  Only wrap solid wire around a terminal screw.  Stranded wire will require fork or ring terminals to be installed (crimped or soldered) on the wire and the terminal screw tightened down on the fork or ring terminal.",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    ""
                ]
            }
        },
