        {
            "id": "18110905",
            "user": "MiscBot",
            "timestamp": "15:30, 29 July 2015",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(15,074 bytes)",
            "change": -266,
            "summary": "(Standardizing some image attributes that have now been built into images automatically; removing hidden intro images)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "deletion",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Select the proper wire size. If originating from an electric panel or fuse box, #12 copper is the minimum size when connecting to a 20 amp circuit breaker or fuse; #14 copper is the minimum size when connecting to a 15 amp circuit breaker or fuse (aluminum wire on circuits of these capacities has been prohibited for many years).  [[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Select the proper cable type.  The power supply or feed cable should be \"2 wire\" (or conductor) cable ''plus'' a ground wire. See below for descriptions and uses of common cable types. [[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Shut power off.  This is a very important step.  Please do not skip it.[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Install a 2 wire cable between the power source (outlet box, electric panel, etc) and the first switch box.  Leave {{convert|8|-|10|in|cm|1}} of wire inside both boxes (source and first switch) before cutting the cable to facilitate easy connection to the switch and power source.  Connect the ground wire to the circuit ground wire(s) with a wirenut or other approved connection (see How to [[Connect Electrical Wire]]). The ground wire should be connected to the neutral terminal bar. Finally, connect the black wire to the hot supply or the circuit breaker / fuse and the white to the neutral supply or neutral terminal bar in the electrical panel.[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Install a 3 wire cable from the first switch box to the light fixture's box.  Leave {{convert|8|-|10|in|cm|1}} of wire inside each of the boxes before cutting the cable to facilitate easy splicing and connection to the switch and fixture. [[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Install a 3 wire cable from the second switch box to the light fixture's box.  Leave {{convert|8|-|10|in|cm|1}} of wire inside each box before cutting the cable to facilitate easy splicing and connection to the fixture.[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Connect ground wires.  Provide as shorter lengths (8 inches/20.5 cm) of bare or green insulated wire to this wire-nutted group to allow connection to green ground terminal screws on each device in the box (switches, outlets, fixtures, etc.) - one length of insulated wire per ground terminal screw. If the switch or junction box is metal, it too ''must be securely grounded'' with a green ground screw or approved grounding clip.  This must be done at each and every box that a cable enters and at each device that provides a termination point for ground. [[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 7 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Connect feed wires in the first switch box.  First, connect all ground wires as previously described.  The 2 wire cable feed from the power source enters the bottom of the switch box and the hot (black) wire connects to the common or shunt terminal on the 3-way switch.  There is only one such terminal on a 3-way switch, and it is usually identified as the one having a different color terminal screw (often significantly darker) from the other two terminal screws (not counting the green ground screw). [[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 8 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Connect 3 wire cable in the first switch box.  The 3 wire cable enters through the top of the first switch box. The red insulated wire connects to either one of the 2 unused terminal screws (shown in the image above to be on the top left and right sides of both 3-way switches).  It does not matter to which terminal screw this wire is connected.[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 9 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Connect wires in the light fixture's box.  Again, connect all ground wires as previously described, if not already completed.  In the light fixture's box, there will be two 3 wire cables. One 3 wire cable originates at the first switch box and has its white insulated wire as a neutral. The other 3 wire cable originates at the second switch box and will become the \"switch leg\". Mark both ends of this wire by wrapping it with black electrical tape to alert others working on the circuit later that it is no longer neutral. This is a newer electrical code requirement, but has been a common practice whenever a white or gray wire is / or can become hot. [[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 10 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Connect 3 wire cable in the second switch box to the switch.  Connect all ground wires as previously described, if not already completed. Connect the black insulated wire to the shunt or common terminal screw of the switch (once again, the common screw terminal is the different colored screw from the rest of those on the switch).[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 11 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Connect the fixture.  The light fixture's box should have only a black wire, a white wire and a ground wire to supply power to the fixture.[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 12 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Finish up.  Check all wire nuts for tightness and exposed neutral and hot conductor surfaces. Fold all wires carefully into the boxes and secure devices and fixture with screws.  Install plates and covers.  Restore power and test.[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 13 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Disconnect circuit power. Make sure there is no power on the circuit.[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 14.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Connect circuit cables to the light. The circuit's Earth (green) and Neutral (black) cables connect to the light's green & blue cables, respectively.[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 15.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Connect the circuit's active (red) wire to switch ONE common terminal (middle). Connect the switching wire (white) to Terminal 1. Connect the second switching wire (white or red) to Terminal 2.[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 16.jpg|center]]",
                    "# On the switch, connect both switching wires (terminal 1 and terminal 2 respectively on switch 2) and the common terminal to the red wire. This then gets connected to the light.[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 17.jpg|center]]",
                    "# At the light fitting, connect switch one's switching wire 1 to switch two's switching wire 1. Then connect switch one's switching wire 2 to switch two's switching wire 2.[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 18.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Connect switch 2's red wire (connected to switch 2's common terminal) to the active terminal on the light fitting (red or brown).[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 19.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Select the proper wire size. If originating from an electric panel or fuse box, #12 copper is the minimum size when connecting to a 20 amp circuit breaker or fuse; #14 copper is the minimum size when connecting to a 15 amp circuit breaker or fuse (aluminum wire on circuits of these capacities has been prohibited for many years).  <br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Select the proper cable type.  The power supply or feed cable should be \"2 wire\" (or conductor) cable ''plus'' a ground wire. See below for descriptions and uses of common cable types. <br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Shut power off.  This is a very important step.  Please do not skip it.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Install a 2 wire cable between the power source (outlet box, electric panel, etc) and the first switch box.  Leave {{convert|8|-|10|in|cm|1}} of wire inside both boxes (source and first switch) before cutting the cable to facilitate easy connection to the switch and power source.  Connect the ground wire to the circuit ground wire(s) with a wirenut or other approved connection (see How to [[Connect Electrical Wire]]). The ground wire should be connected to the neutral terminal bar. Finally, connect the black wire to the hot supply or the circuit breaker / fuse and the white to the neutral supply or neutral terminal bar in the electrical panel.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Install a 3 wire cable from the first switch box to the light fixture's box.  Leave {{convert|8|-|10|in|cm|1}} of wire inside each of the boxes before cutting the cable to facilitate easy splicing and connection to the switch and fixture. <br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Install a 3 wire cable from the second switch box to the light fixture's box.  Leave {{convert|8|-|10|in|cm|1}} of wire inside each box before cutting the cable to facilitate easy splicing and connection to the fixture.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Connect ground wires.  Provide as shorter lengths (8 inches/20.5 cm) of bare or green insulated wire to this wire-nutted group to allow connection to green ground terminal screws on each device in the box (switches, outlets, fixtures, etc.) - one length of insulated wire per ground terminal screw. If the switch or junction box is metal, it too ''must be securely grounded'' with a green ground screw or approved grounding clip.  This must be done at each and every box that a cable enters and at each device that provides a termination point for ground. <br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 7 Version 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Connect feed wires in the first switch box.  First, connect all ground wires as previously described.  The 2 wire cable feed from the power source enters the bottom of the switch box and the hot (black) wire connects to the common or shunt terminal on the 3-way switch.  There is only one such terminal on a 3-way switch, and it is usually identified as the one having a different color terminal screw (often significantly darker) from the other two terminal screws (not counting the green ground screw). <br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 8 Version 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Connect 3 wire cable in the first switch box.  The 3 wire cable enters through the top of the first switch box. The red insulated wire connects to either one of the 2 unused terminal screws (shown in the image above to be on the top left and right sides of both 3-way switches).  It does not matter to which terminal screw this wire is connected.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 9 Version 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Connect wires in the light fixture's box.  Again, connect all ground wires as previously described, if not already completed.  In the light fixture's box, there will be two 3 wire cables. One 3 wire cable originates at the first switch box and has its white insulated wire as a neutral. The other 3 wire cable originates at the second switch box and will become the \"switch leg\". Mark both ends of this wire by wrapping it with black electrical tape to alert others working on the circuit later that it is no longer neutral. This is a newer electrical code requirement, but has been a common practice whenever a white or gray wire is / or can become hot. <br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 10 Version 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Connect 3 wire cable in the second switch box to the switch.  Connect all ground wires as previously described, if not already completed. Connect the black insulated wire to the shunt or common terminal screw of the switch (once again, the common screw terminal is the different colored screw from the rest of those on the switch).<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 11 Version 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Connect the fixture.  The light fixture's box should have only a black wire, a white wire and a ground wire to supply power to the fixture.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 12 Version 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Finish up.  Check all wire nuts for tightness and exposed neutral and hot conductor surfaces. Fold all wires carefully into the boxes and secure devices and fixture with screws.  Install plates and covers.  Restore power and test.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 13 Version 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Disconnect circuit power. Make sure there is no power on the circuit.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 14.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Connect circuit cables to the light. The circuit's Earth (green) and Neutral (black) cables connect to the light's green & blue cables, respectively.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 15.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Connect the circuit's active (red) wire to switch ONE common terminal (middle). Connect the switching wire (white) to Terminal 1. Connect the second switching wire (white or red) to Terminal 2.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 16.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# On the switch, connect both switching wires (terminal 1 and terminal 2 respectively on switch 2) and the common terminal to the red wire. This then gets connected to the light.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 17.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# At the light fitting, connect switch one's switching wire 1 to switch two's switching wire 1. Then connect switch one's switching wire 2 to switch two's switching wire 2.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 18.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Connect switch 2's red wire (connected to switch 2's common terminal) to the active terminal on the light fitting (red or brown).<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 19.jpg|center|550px]]"
                ]
            }
        },
