        {
            "id": "16045349",
            "user": "Votebot",
            "timestamp": "15:01, 2 December 2014",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(15,643 bytes)",
            "change": 0,
            "summary": "(reordering tips and warnings based on votes)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "adjustment",
            "reverted_by": "Anna",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "*A 120V / 15A circuit is rated to support up to 1440 watts of continuous load (heat, lights, etc.), so you'd need quite a few lights to max-out a 15A / #14 circuit.  For comparison, a 120V / 20A circuit is rated to support up to 1920 watts of continuous load (heat, lights, etc.). If a larger load is to be connected, the appropriately larger sized wire and circuit breaker or fuse must be installed instead.",
                    "**The circuit's maximum design load - in this case watts - is determined by Volts X Amps X .80, where volts and amps are given and .80 is required by the code to de-rate the circuit capacity to 80% of maximum.  It could be said that the maximum amperage of a 15 amp circuit is 12 amps by applying a similar formula: Circuit Breaker / Fuse X .80 = maximum amp load. The same for a 20 amp circuit: 20 X .80 = 16 amps."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "*A 120V / 15A circuit is rated to support up to 1440 watts of continuous load (heat, lights, etc.), so you'd need quite a few lights to max-out a 15A / #14 circuit.  For comparison, a 120V / 20A circuit is rated to support up to 1920 watts of continuous load (heat, lights, etc.). If a larger load is to be connected, the appropriately larger sized wire and circuit breaker or fuse must be installed instead.",
                    "**The circuit's maximum design load - in this case watts - is determined by Volts X Amps X .80, where volts and amps are given and .80 is required by the code to de-rate the circuit capacity to 80% of maximum.  It could be said that the maximum amperage of a 15 amp circuit is 12 amps by applying a similar formula: Circuit Breaker / Fuse X .80 = maximum amp load. The same for a 20 amp circuit: 20 X .80 = 16 amps."
                ]
            }
        },
