            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "deletion",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "Here is a detailed explanation of one of the hardest electrical circuits to figure out. This is one of the easiest ways to do 3-way light switch wiring.  Be sure to check the \"Tips\" section to see several other accepted ways to wire this circuit.",
                    "#[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 1 Version 2.jpg|right|300px]]Select the proper wire size.  The size of all the wires in any circuit ''must'' be the same size.  If originating from an electric panel or fuse box, #12 copper is the minimum size when connecting to a 20 amp circuit breaker or fuse; #14 copper is the minimum size when connecting to a 15 amp circuit breaker or fuse (it has not been permissible to use aluminum wire on circuits of these capacities for many years).  If obtaining power from a nearby electrical outlet, etc.; the new wiring must be the same size as those that supply the outlet or other circuit device.",
                    "#[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 2 Version 2.jpg|right|300px]]Select the proper cable type.  The power supply or feed cable should be \"2 wire\" (or conductor) cable ''plus'' a ground wire.  Common cable types with these features are:",
                    "#[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 3 Version 2.jpg|right|300px]]Shut power off.  This is a very important step.  Please do not skip it.",
                    "#[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 4 Version 2.jpg|right|300px]]Install a 2 wire cable between the power source (outlet box, electric panel, etc.) to the first switch box.  Leave 8 - 10 inches of wire inside both boxes (source and first switch) before cutting the cable to facilitate easy connection to the switch and power source.  Add the ground wire to the circuit ground wire(s) with a wirenut connection.  If the power source is an electrical panel or fuse box, the cable should be cut at least long enough to reach the furthest termination point (breaker or fuse, ground and neutral bar) without the need of splicing.  The ground wire should be connected to either 1) the neutral terminal bar or, 2) the ground terminal bar (but only if equipped with a separate ground bar).  If all the existing ground wires connect to one bar and all the existing white wires connect to a different bar, maintaining separate ground and neutral connections is required.  ''Never'' introduce ground wire(s) to a terminal bar with only white or gray insulated wires connected or vice-versa.  Connect the black wire to the hot supply or the circuit breaker / fuse and the white to the neutral supply or neutral terminal bar in the electrical panel.",
                    "#[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 5 Version 2.jpg|right|300px]]Install a 3 wire cable from the first switch box to the light fixture's box.  Leave 8 - 10 inches of wire inside each of the boxes before cutting the cable to facilitate easy splicing and connection to the switch and fixture.  A 3 wire cable has an \"extra\" wire when compared to 2 wire cables, and this wire is nearly always covered in red insulation.  This third wire is necessary for 3-way switch installations.",
                    "#[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 6 Version 2.jpg|right|300px]]Install a 3 wire cable from the second switch box to the light fixture's box.  Leave 8 - 10 inches of wire inside each box before cutting the cable to facilitate easy splicing and connection to the fixture.",
                    "#[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 7 Version 2.jpg|right|300px]]Connect ground wires.  All the ground wires in any electrical box or enclosure must be connected together by wire nut, lug or other approved method.  Provide as many shorter lengths (8 inch) of bare or green insulated wire as needed (one for each) to this wire-nutted group to allow connection to green ground terminal screws on each device in the box (switches, outlets, fixtures, etc.). If the switch or junction box is metal, it too ''must be securely grounded'' with a green ground screw or approved grounding clip.  This must be done at each and every box that a cable enters and at each device that provides a termination point for ground.  It is highly recommended that these ground connections be completed first, then gently folded into the rear of the box - out of the way - leaving only the shorter ground leads out for easy connection to devices.  No ground connection is made to plastic, fiber or other non-conductive box.",
                    "#[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 8 Version 2.jpg|right|300px]]Connect feed wires in the first switch box.  Connect all ground wires as previously described.  The 2 wire cable feed from the power source enters the bottom of the switch box and the hot (black) wire connects to the common or shunt terminal on the 3-way switch.  There is only one such terminal on a 3-way switch.  This terminal is usually identified as the one having a different color terminal screw (often significantly darker) from the other two terminal screws (not counting the green ground screw).  In the picture of the circuit above, the shunt or common terminal is on the lower right side both of the switches.",
                    "#[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 9 Version 2.jpg|right|300px]]Connect 3 wire cable in the first switch box.  The 3 wire cable enters through the top of the first switch box. The red insulated wire connects to either one of the 2 unused terminal screws (shown in the image above to be on the top left and right sides of both 3-way switches).  It does not matter to which terminal screw this wire is connected.",
                    "#[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 10 Version 2.jpg|right|300px]]Connect wires in the light fixture's box.  Connect all ground wires as previously described, if not already completed.  In the light fixture's box, there will be two 3 wire cables. One 3 wire cable originates at the first switch box and has its white insulated wire as a neutral; the other 3 wire cable originates at the second switch box and its white will become what is called the \"switch leg\".",
                    "#[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 11 Version 2.jpg|right|300px]]Connect 3 wire cable in the second switch box to the switch.  Connect all ground wires as previously described, if not already completed. Connect the black insulated wire to the shunt or common terminal screw of the switch (once again, the common screw terminal is the different colored screw from the rest of those on the switch).",
                    "#[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 12 Version 2.jpg|right|300px]]Connect the fixture.  The light fixture's box should have only a black wire, a white wire and a ground wire to supply power to the fixture.",
                    "#[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 13 Version 2.jpg|right|300px]]Finish up.  Check all wire nuts for tightness and exposed neutral and hot conductor surfaces. Fold all wires carefully into the boxes and secure devices and fixture with screws.  Install plates and covers.  Restore power and test.",
                    "# [[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 14.jpg|right|300px]]Disconnect circuit power (and check no power on circuit)",
                    "# [[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 15.jpg|right|300px]]Connect circuit Earth (Green) and circuit Neutral (Black)to light (Green & Blue respectively).",
                    "# [[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 16.jpg|right|300px]]Connect circuit Active (Red) to switch ONE common terminal (middle); Connect Switching Wire (White) to Terminal 1; Connect second switching wire (white or red) to terminal 2.",
                    "# [[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 17.jpg|right|300px]]On switch both connect switching wires (terminal 1 and terminal 2 respectively on switch 2) and the common terminal to Red Wire (this then gets connected to light).",
                    "# [[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 18.jpg|right|300px]]At light fitting connect switch one-switching wire 1 to switch two switching wire 1; and connect switch one-switching wire 2 to switch two-switching wire 2.",
                    "# [[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 19.jpg|right|300px]]Connect switch 2 red wire (connected to switch 2 common terminal) to active terminal on light fitting (Red or Brown).",
                    "*One terminal = one wire.  It is not permissible to connect more than one wire under a screw terminal. Additionally, wire should wrap clockwise around the screw.  Only solid wire is to be wrapped around a terminal screw.  Stranded wire will require installation of fork or ring terminals to be installed (crimped or soldered) on the wire and the terminal screw tightened down on the fork or ring terminal.",
                    "*Use of screw terminals is preferable to the \"back wire-able\" option provided on some switches and outlets that allow for simple insertion of a stripped wire into a hole for connection without the need of turning a screw.  Over time, these pressure connections fatigue and eventually fail.",
                    "*If the power comes from different areas of the house, it can effect how you diagnose problems with the switches.   This first of three methods is often used when extending a power source - such as an outlet  - that is near one switch or the other.  This is also the method described in step-by-step detail in the main article above.",
                    "*This second method is used when switches are far apart and the fixture is somewhere between them - such as when switches are located at the top and bottom of stairs and the fixture is located closer to one of them.  It is also the only way to upgrade an existing pull-chain operated fixture so that it can be controlled by two 3-way switches.",
                    "*This third method is used when the switches are relatively close to each other, but the fixture is some distance away.  An example: switches at two doorways on the same wall to the same room that control a fixture in the middle."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Intro.jpg|right|300px]]Here is a detailed explanation of one of the hardest electrical circuits to figure out. This is one of the easiest ways to do 3-way light switch wiring.  Be sure to check the \"Tips\" section to see several other accepted ways to wire this circuit.",
                    "#Select the proper wire size.  The size of all the wires in any circuit ''must'' be the same size.  If originating from an electric panel or fuse box, #12 copper is the minimum size when connecting to a 20 amp circuit breaker or fuse; #14 copper is the minimum size when connecting to a 15 amp circuit breaker or fuse (it has not been permissible to use aluminum wire on circuits of these capacities for many years).  If obtaining power from a nearby electrical outlet, etc.; the new wiring must be the same size as those that supply the outlet or other circuit device.{{largeimage|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 1.jpg}}",
                    "#Select the proper cable type.  The power supply or feed cable should be \"2 wire\" (or conductor) cable ''plus'' a ground wire.  Common cable types with these features are:{{largeimage|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 2.jpg}}",
                    "#Shut power off.  This is a very important step.  Please do not skip it.{{largeimage|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 3.jpg}}",
                    "#Install a 2 wire cable between the power source (outlet box, electric panel, etc.) to the first switch box.  Leave 8 - 10 inches of wire inside both boxes (source and first switch) before cutting the cable to facilitate easy connection to the switch and power source.  Add the ground wire to the circuit ground wire(s) with a wirenut connection.  If the power source is an electrical panel or fuse box, the cable should be cut at least long enough to reach the furthest termination point (breaker or fuse, ground and neutral bar) without the need of splicing.  The ground wire should be connected to either 1) the neutral terminal bar or, 2) the ground terminal bar (but only if equipped with a separate ground bar).  If all the existing ground wires connect to one bar and all the existing white wires connect to a different bar, maintaining separate ground and neutral connections is required.  ''Never'' introduce ground wire(s) to a terminal bar with only white or gray insulated wires connected or vice-versa.  Connect the black wire to the hot supply or the circuit breaker / fuse and the white to the neutral supply or neutral terminal bar in the electrical panel. {{largeimage|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 4.jpg}}",
                    "#Install a 3 wire cable from the first switch box to the light fixture's box.  Leave 8 - 10 inches of wire inside each of the boxes before cutting the cable to facilitate easy splicing and connection to the switch and fixture.  A 3 wire cable has an \"extra\" wire when compared to 2 wire cables, and this wire is nearly always covered in red insulation.  This third wire is necessary for 3-way switch installations.{{largeimage|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 5.jpg}}",
                    "#Install a 3 wire cable from the second switch box to the light fixture's box.  Leave 8 - 10 inches of wire inside each box before cutting the cable to facilitate easy splicing and connection to the fixture.{{largeimage|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 6.jpg}}",
                    "#Connect ground wires.  All the ground wires in any electrical box or enclosure must be connected together by wire nut, lug or other approved method.  Provide as many shorter lengths (8 inch) of bare or green insulated wire as needed (one for each) to this wire-nutted group to allow connection to green ground terminal screws on each device in the box (switches, outlets, fixtures, etc.). If the switch or junction box is metal, it too ''must be securely grounded'' with a green ground screw or approved grounding clip.  This must be done at each and every box that a cable enters and at each device that provides a termination point for ground.  It is highly recommended that these ground connections be completed first, then gently folded into the rear of the box - out of the way - leaving only the shorter ground leads out for easy connection to devices.  No ground connection is made to plastic, fiber or other non-conductive box.{{largeimage|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 7.jpg}}",
                    "#Connect feed wires in the first switch box.  Connect all ground wires as previously described.  The 2 wire cable feed from the power source enters the bottom of the switch box and the hot (black) wire connects to the common or shunt terminal on the 3-way switch.  There is only one such terminal on a 3-way switch.  This terminal is usually identified as the one having a different color terminal screw (often significantly darker) from the other two terminal screws (not counting the green ground screw).  In the picture of the circuit above, the shunt or common terminal is on the lower right side both of the switches.{{largeimage|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 8.jpg}}",
                    "#Connect 3 wire cable in the first switch box.  The 3 wire cable enters through the top of the first switch box. The red insulated wire connects to either one of the 2 unused terminal screws (shown in the image above to be on the top left and right sides of both 3-way switches).  It does not matter to which terminal screw this wire is connected.{{largeimage|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 9.jpg}}",
                    "#Connect wires in the light fixture's box.  Connect all ground wires as previously described, if not already completed.  In the light fixture's box, there will be two 3 wire cables. One 3 wire cable originates at the first switch box and has its white insulated wire as a neutral; the other 3 wire cable originates at the second switch box and its white will become what is called the \"switch leg\".{{largeimage|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 10.jpg}}",
                    "#Connect 3 wire cable in the second switch box to the switch.  Connect all ground wires as previously described, if not already completed. Connect the black insulated wire to the shunt or common terminal screw of the switch (once again, the common screw terminal is the different colored screw from the rest of those on the switch).{{largeimage|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 11.jpg}}",
                    "#Connect the fixture.  The light fixture's box should have only a black wire, a white wire and a ground wire to supply power to the fixture.{{largeimage|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 12.jpg}}",
                    "#Finish up.  Check all wire nuts for tightness and exposed neutral and hot conductor surfaces. Fold all wires carefully into the boxes and secure devices and fixture with screws.  Install plates and covers.  Restore power and test.{{largeimage|Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 13.jpg}}",
                    "# Disconnect circuit power (and check no power on circuit)",
                    "# Connect circuit Earth (Green) and circuit Neutral (Black)to light (Green & Blue respectively).",
                    "# Connect circuit Active (Red) to switch ONE common terminal (middle); Connect Switching Wire (White) to Terminal 1; Connect second switching wire (white or red) to terminal 2.",
                    "# On switch both connect switching wires (terminal 1 and terminal 2 respectively on switch 2) and the common terminal to Red Wire (this then gets connected to light).",
                    "# At light fitting connect switch one-switching wire 1 to switch two switching wire 1; and connect switch one-switching wire 2 to switch two-switching wire 2.",
                    "# Connect switch 2 red wire (connected to switch 2 common terminal) to active terminal on light fitting (Red or Brown).",
                    "*[[Image:Fuse panel wiring by clowns..jpg|right|184px|thumb|A terminal strip with 10 wires connected that have fork terminals crimped on the ends.  Crimped terminals are easily and quickly installed.  They prevent stray strands of wire from coming in contact with something unintended and make it easy to connect stranded wires to screw terminals. ]]One terminal = one wire.  It is not permissible to connect more than one wire under a screw terminal. Additionally, wire should wrap clockwise around the screw.  Only solid wire is to be wrapped around a terminal screw.  Stranded wire will require installation of fork or ring terminals to be installed (crimped or soldered) on the wire and the terminal screw tightened down on the fork or ring terminal.",
                    "*[[Image:Backwire.jpg|right|184px|thumb|This is a back wire-able 15 amp duplex outlet.  There are \"stab\" openings designed to accept a #14 solid copper wire.  The internal connection behind the stab opening relies on compression from a piece of spring metal against the wire that is significantly inferior to the screw terminals.  Resist using these back wireable openings.]]Use of screw terminals is preferable to the \"back wire-able\" option provided on some switches and outlets that allow for simple insertion of a stripped wire into a hole for connection without the need of turning a screw.  Over time, these pressure connections fatigue and eventually fail.",
                    "*[[Image:3_Way_Switches_3.jpg|right|184px|thumb|Method #1.  Power from Switch to Light to Switch]]If the power comes from different areas of the house, it can effect how you diagnose problems with the switches.   This first of three methods is often used when extending a power source - such as an outlet  - that is near one switch or the other.  This is also the method described in step-by-step detail in the main article above.",
                    "*[[Image:3_Way_Switches_2.jpg|right|184px|thumb|Method #2.  Power from Light to Switch to Switch]]This second method is used when switches are far apart and the fixture is somewhere between them - such as when switches are located at the top and bottom of stairs and the fixture is located closer to one of them.  It is also the only way to upgrade an existing pull-chain operated fixture so that it can be controlled by two 3-way switches.",
                    "*[[Image:3_Way_Switches_1.jpg|right|184px|thumb|Method #3. Power from Switch to Switch to Light]]This third method is used when the switches are relatively close to each other, but the fixture is some distance away.  An example: switches at two doorways on the same wall to the same room that control a fixture in the middle."
                ]
            }
        },
