                "added": [
                    "*[[Image:3_Way_Switches_3.jpg|right|184px|thumb|Method #1.  Power from Switch to Light to Switch]]If the power comes from different areas of the house, it can effect how you diagnose problems with the switches.   This first of three methods is often used when extending a power source - such as an outlet  - that is near one switch or the other.  This is also the method described in step-by-step detail in the main article above.",
                    "*[[Image:3_Way_Switches_2.jpg|right|184px|thumb|Method #2.  Power from Light to Switch to Switch]]This second method is used when switches are far apart and the fixture is somewhere between them - such as when switches are located at the top and bottom of stairs and the fixture is located closer to one of them.  It is also the only way to upgrade an existing pull-chain operated fixture so that it can be controlled by two 3-way switches.",
                    "",
                    "*[[Image:3_Way_Switches_1.jpg|right|184px|thumb|Method #3. Power from Switch to Switch to Light]]This third method is used when the switches are relatively close to each other, but the fixture is some distance away.  An example: switches at two doorways on the same wall to the same room that control a fixture in the middle."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "*If the power comes from different areas of the house, it can effect how you diagnose problems with the switches.   This first of three methods is often used when extending a power source - such as an outlet  - that is near one switch or the other.  This is also the method described in step-by-step detail in the main article above.",
                    "[[Image:3_Way_Switches_3.jpg|right|184px|thumb|Method #1.  Power from Switch to Light to Switch]]",
                    "*This second method is used when switches are far apart and the fixture is somewhere between them - such as when switches are located at the top and bottom of stairs and the fixture is located closer to one of them.  It is also the only way to upgrade an existing pull-chain operated fixture so that it can be controlled by two 3-way switches.  [[Image:3_Way_Switches_2.jpg|right|184px|thumb|Method #2.  Power from Light to Switch to Switch]]",
                    "*This third method is used when the switches are relatively close to each other, but the fixture is some distance away.  An example: switches at two doorways on the same wall to the same room that control a fixture in the middle. [[Image:3_Way_Switches_1.jpg|right|184px|thumb|Method #3. Power from Switch to Switch to Light]]"
                ]
