            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "*[[Image:Backwire.jpg|right|184px|thumb|This is a back wire-able 15 amp duplex outlet.  There are \"stab\" openings designed to accept a #14 solid copper wire.  The internal connection behind the stab opening relies on compression from a piece of spring metal against the wire that is significantly inferior to the screw terminals.  Resist using these back wireable openings.]]Use of screw terminals is preferable to the \"back wire-able\" option provided on some switches and outlets that allow for simple insertion of a stripped wire into a hole for connection without the need of turning a screw.  Over time, these pressure connections fatigue and eventually fail.",
                    "*If the power comes from different areas of the house, it can effect how you diagnose problems with the switches. Here are 3 diagrams of those possibilities. [[Image:3_Way_Switches_3.jpg|right|184px|thumb|Power from Switch to Light to Switch]][[Image:3_Way_Switches_2.jpg|right|184px|thumb|Power from Light to Switch to Switch]][[Image:3_Way_Switches_1.jpg|right|184px|thumb|Power from Switch to Switch to Light]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "*[[Image:Backwire.jpg|right|184px|thumb|This is a back wireable 15 amp duplex outlet.  There are stab openings designed to accept a #14 solid copper wire.  The internal connection behind the stab opening relies on compression from a piece of spring metal against the wire that is significantly inferior to the screw terminals.  Resist using these back wireable openings.]]Use of screw terminals is preferable to the \"back wire-able\" devices that allow for simple insertion of a stripped wire into a hole for connection without the need of turning a screw.  Over time, these pressure connections fatigue and eventually fail.",
                    "*If the power comes from different areas of the house, it can effect how you diagnosis problems with the switches. Here are 3 diagrams of those possibilities. [[Image:3_Way_Switches_3.jpg|right|184px|thumb|Power from Switch to Light to Switch]][[Image:3_Way_Switches_2.jpg|right|184px|thumb|Power from Light to Switch to Switch]][[Image:3_Way_Switches_1.jpg|right|184px|thumb|Power from Switch to Switch to Light]]"
                ]
            }
        },
