                "added": [
                    "#Select the proper wire size.  The size of all the wires in any circuit ''must'' be the same size.  If originating from an electric panel, #12 copper is the minimum size when connecting to a 20 amp circuit breaker or fuse; #14 copper is the minimum size when connecting to a 15 amp circuit breaker or fuse (it has not been permissible to use aluminum wire on circuits of these capacities for many years).  If obtaining power from a nearby electrical outlet, etc.; the new wiring must be the same size as those that supply the outlet or other circuit device.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Select the proper cable type.  The power supply or feed cable should be \"2 wire\" (or conductor) cable ''plus'' a ground wire.  Common cable types with these features are:<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#*BX, MC and AC types are armored cables.  They are very similar with only slight variations (construction consists of a metal jacket formed by an interlocking, helically wound band of steel or aluminum around two [or more] insulated wires - one white, one black and others including a green or bare wire). Cables lacking a green insulated or a bare wire utilize the outer metal jacket as the ground conductor. None of these metal jacketed cables types are permitted to be installed outside or underground.  If the power source is from an armored type cable that lacks a full size (#12 or #14) ground wire, a metal box must be used to extend the ground from the armored shell to the box and to the extended circuit's ground wire via special green, hex headed grounding machine screw that is threaded into the pre-tapped hole in the metal box, or special green ground clip.",
                    "#*All of these cables have \"trade names\" which are derived from the number of insulated non-ground conductors and construction type; such as: \"twelve two Romex\" or \"fourteen three BX\".  A 12/2 NM, BX, AC, or Romex cable has two #12 conductors, plus a full sized #12 ground.  A 14/3 NM, BX, AC or Romex cable has three #14 conductors , plus a full sized #14 ground.  There are special connectors for armored cables and unique precautions and instructions for each.  Use of the similar in appearance Romex connector on an armored cable is not permissible.  NM or Romex type cable is easier to work and costs less to purchase and as such; is extensively use.",
                    "#Install a 2 wire cable between the power source (outlet box, electric panel, etc.) to the first switch box.  Leave 8 - 10 inches of wires inside the boxes before cutting the cable to facilitate easy connection to the switch.  Add the ground wire to the circuit ground wire(s) with a wirenut connection.  If the power source is an electrical panel, the ground wire should be connected to either 1)the neutral terminal bar or, 2)the ground terminal bar (but only if equipped with a separate ground bar).  If all the existing ground wires connect to one bar and all the existing white wires connect to a different bar, maintain separate ground and neutral connection.  Never introduce ground wire(s) to the terminal bar with white or gray insulated wires connected or vice-versa.  Connect the black wire to the hot supply or the circuit breaker / fuse and the white to the neutral supply or neutral terminal bar in the electrical panel. <br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 4.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Install a 3 wire cable from the first switch box to the light fixture's box.  Leave 8 - 10 inches of wires inside the boxes before cutting the cable to facilitate easy splicing and connection to the fixture.  A 3 wire cable has an \"extra\" wire when compared to 2 wire cables, and this wire is nearly always covered in red insulation.  This third wire is necessary for 3-way switch installations.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 5.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Install a 3 wire cable from the second switch box to the light fixture's box.  Leave 8 - 10 inches of wires inside the boxes before cutting the cable to facilitate easy splicing and connection to the fixture.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 6.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Connect ground wires.  All ground wires in any electrical box or enclosure must be connected together by wire nut, lug or other approved method.  Provide as many shorter lengths (8 inch) of bare or green insulated wire as needed to this wire nut group to allow connection to green ground terminal screws on each device in the box (switches, outlets, fixtures, etc.). If the switch or junction box is metal, it too ''must be securely grounded'' with a green ground screw or approved clip.  This must be done at each and every box that a cable enters and for each device that provides a termination point for ground.  It is highly recommended that these ground connections be completed first, then gently folded into the rear of the box - out of the way - leaving only the shorter ground leads out for easy connection to devices.  No ground connection is made to plastic, fiber or other non-conductive box.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 7.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Connect feed wires in the first switch box.  Connect all ground wires as previously described.  The 2 wire cable feed from the power source enters the bottom of the switch box and the hot (black) wire connects to the common or shunt terminal on the 3-way switch.  This terminal is usually identified by having a different color terminal screw (often darker) from the other two terminal screws (not counting the green ground screw).  In the picture of the circuit above, these terminals are on the lower right side of the switches.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 8.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#*Connect the 3 wire cable's white insulated wire (neutral) directly to the 2 wire 'feed' white insulated (neutral) wire with wire nuts (there is no connection of any white wires to this switch).",
                    "#Connect 3 wire cable in the first switch box.  The 3 wire cable enters through the top of the first switch box. The red insulated wire connects to either one of the 2 unused terminal screws (shown in the image above to be on the top left and right sides of both 3-way switches).  It does not matter to which terminal screw this wire is connected.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 9.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Connect wires in the light fixture's box.  Connect all ground wires as previously described.  In the light fixture's box, there will be two 3 wire cables. One 3 wire cable originates at the first switch box that has the white insulated wire as a neutral; the other 3 wire cable originates at the second switch box and its white will become what is called the \"switch leg\".<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 10.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Connect 3 wire cable in the second switch box to the switch.  Connect all ground wires as previously described. Connect the black insulated wire to the shunt or common terminal screw of the switch (once again, the common screw terminal is the different colored screw from the rest on the switch).<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 11.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Finish up.  Check all wire nuts for tightness and exposed neutral and hot conductor surfaces. Fold all wires carefully into the boxes and secure devices and fixture with screws.  Install plates and covers.  Restore power and test.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 13.jpg|center|550px]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Select the proper wire size.  The size of all the wires in the circuit must be the same size.  If originating from an electric panel, #12 copper is the minimum size when connecting to a 20 amp circuit breaker or fuse; #14 copper is the minimum size when connecting to a 15 amp circuit breaker or fuse (it has not been permissible to use aluminum wire on circuits of these capacities for many years).  If obtaining power from a nearby electrical outlet, etc.; the new wiring must be the same size as those that supply the outlet or other circuit device.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Select the proper cable type.  The power supply or feed cable should be \"2 wire\" (or conductor) cable plus a ground wire.  Common cable types with these features are:<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#*BX, MC and AC types are armored cables.  They are very similar with only slight variations (construction consists of a metal jacket formed by an interlocking, helically wound band of steel or aluminum around two [or more] insulated wires - one white, one black and others including a green or bare wire). Cables lacking a green insulated or a bare wire utilize the outer metal jacket as the ground conductor. None of these metal jacketed cables types are permitted to be installed outside or underground.  If the power source is from an armored type cable that lacks a full size (#12 or #14) ground wire, a metal box must be used to extend the ground from the armored shell to the box and to the rest of the new circuit.",
                    "#*All of these cables have \"trade names\" derived from the number of insulated non-ground conductors and construction; such as: \"twelve two Romex\" or \"fourteen three BX\".  A 12/2 NM, BX, AC, or Romex cable has two #12 conductors, plus a full sized #12 ground.  A 14/3 NM, BX, AC or Romex cable has three #14 conductors , plus a full sized #14 ground.  There are special connectors for armored cables and unique precautions and instructions for each.  Use of the similar in appearance Romex connector on an armored cable is not permissible.  NM or Romex type cable is easier to work and costs less to purchase and as such; is extensively use.",
                    "#Install a 2 wire cable between the power source (outlet box, electric panel, etc.) to the first switch box.  Allow 8 - 10 inches of wires inside the boxes before cutting the cable.  Connect the black wire to the hot supply or the circuit breaker / fuse and the white to the neutral supply or neutral terminal bar in the electrical panel. Finally, add the ground wire to the circuit ground wire(s) with a wirenut connection.  If the power source is an electrical panel, the ground wire should be connected to either 1)the neutral terminal bar or, 2)the ground terminal bar (but only if equipped with a separate ground bar).  If all the existing ground wires connect to one bar and all the existing white wires connect to a different bar, maintain separate ground and neutral connection.  Never introduce ground wire(s) to the terminal bar with white or gray insulated wires connected or vice-versa.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 4.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Install a 3 wire cable from the first switch box to the light fixture's box.  Allow 8 - 10 inches of wires inside the boxes before cutting the cable.  A 3 wire cable has an \"extra\" wire when compared to 2 wire cables, and this wire is nearly always covered in red insulation.  This third wire is necessary for 3-way switch installations.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 5.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Install a 3 wire cable from the second switch box to the light fixture's box.  Allow 8 - 10 inches of wires inside the boxes before cutting the cable.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 6.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Connect ground wires.  All ground wires in any box or enclosure must be connected together by wire nut, lug or other approved method.  Provide as many shorter lengths (8 inch) of bare or green insulated wire as needed to this wire nut group to allow connection to green ground terminal screws on each device in the box (switches, outlets, fixtures, etc.). If the switch or junction box is metal, it too must be securely grounded with a green ground screw or approved clip.  This must be done at each and every box that a cable enters and for each device that provides a termination point for ground.  It is highly recommended that these ground connections be completed first, then gently folded into the rear of the box - out of the way - leaving only the shorter ground leads out for easy connection to devices.  No ground connection is made to plastic or fiber boxes.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 7.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Connect feed wires in the first switch box. The 2 wire cable feed from the power source enters the bottom of the switch box and the hot (black) wire connects to the common or shunt terminal on the 3-way switch.  This terminal is usually identified by having a different color terminal screw (often darker) from the other two terminal screws (and the green ground screw).  In the picture of the circuit above, these terminals are on the lower right side of the switches.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 8.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#*Connect the 3 wire cable's white insulated wire (neutral) directly to the 2 wire 'feed' white insulated (neutral) wire with wire nuts.  There is no connection of any white wires to this switch.",
                    "#Connect 3 wire cable in the first switch box to the switch.  The 3 wire cable enters through the top of the first switch box. The red insulated wire connects to one of the 2 unused terminal screws (shown in the image above to be on the top left and right sides of both 3-way switches).  It does not matter to which terminal screw the wire is connected.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 9.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Connect wires in the light fixture's box.  In the light fixture's box, there will be two 3 wire cables. One 3 wire cable originates at the first switch box that has the white insulated wire as a neutral; the other 3 wire cable originates at the second switch box and its white will become what is called the \"switch leg\".<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 10.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Connect 3 wire cable in the second switch box to the switch.  Connect the black insulated wire to the shunt or common terminal screw of the switch (once again, the common screw terminal is the different colored screw from the rest on the switch).<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 11.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Finish up.  Check wire nuts for tightness and exposed neutral and hot conductor surfaces. Fold all wires carefully into the boxes and secure devices and fixture with screws.  Install plates and covers.  Restore power and test.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 13.jpg|center|550px]]"
                ]
            }
        },
