                    "#*BX, MC and AC types are \"armored\" cables.  They are very similar with only slight variations (construction consists of a metal jacket formed by an interlocking, helically wound band of steel or aluminum around two [or more] insulated wires - one white, one black and others including a green or bare wire). Cables lacking a green insulated or a bare wire utilize the outer metal jacket as the ground conductor. None of these metal jacketed cables types are permitted to be installed outside or underground.",
                    "#*All of these cables have \"trade names\" derived from the number of insulated non-ground conductors and construction; such as: \"twelve two Romex\" or \"fourteen three BX\".  A 12/2 NM, BX, AC, or Romex cable has two #12 conductors, plus a ground.  A 14/3 NM, BX, AC or Romex cable has three #14 conductors , plus a ground.  There are special connectors for armored cables and unique precautions and instructions for each.  Use of the similar in appearance Romex connector on an armored cable is not permissible.  NM or Romex type cable is easier to work and costs less to purchase and as such is extensively use.",
