            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "",
                    "#Select the proper wire size.  The size of all the wires in the circuit must be the same size.  If originating from an electric panel, #12 copper is the minimum size if connecting to a 20 amp circuit breaker or fuse and #14 copper is the minimum size if connecting to a 15 amp circuit breaker or fuse (it has not been permissible to use aluminum wire on circuits of these capacities for many years).  If obtaining power from a nearby electrical outlet, etc.; the new wiring must be the same size as those that supply the outlet or other circuit device.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Select the proper cable type.  The power supply or feed cable should be 2 wire or conductor cable plus a ground wire.  Common cable types with these features are:<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Shut power off.  This is a very important step.  Please do not skip it.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 3.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Install a 2 wire cable between the power source (outlet box, electric panel, etc.) to the first switch box.  Connect the black wire to the hot supply or circuit breaker / fuse and the white to the neutral supply to the neutral terminal bar in the panel. If connecting in an electrical panel, a the ground wire should be connected to either the neutral terminal bar or the ground terminal bar (if equipped).  If all the existing ground wires connect to one bar and all the existing white wires connect to the other, do not introduce white wires to the terminal bar with bare or green insulated wires and vice-versa.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 4.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Install a 3 wire cable from the first switch box to the light fixture's box.  A 3 wire cable has an \"extra\" wire when compared to 2 wire cables, and is nearly always coated in red insulation.  This third wire is necessary for 3-way switch installations.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 5.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Install a 3 wire cable from the second switch box to the light fixture's box.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 6.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Connect ground wires.  All ground wires in any box or enclosure must be connected together by wire nut, lug or other approved method.  Provide as many shorter lengths (8 inch) of bare or green insulated wire as needed to this wire nut group to allow connections to green ground terminal screws on each device in the box (switches, outlets, fixtures, etc.) and if the box is metal, it too must be grounded with a green ground screw or approved clip.  No connection is made to plastic or fiber boxes.  This must be done at each and every box and device that the cable enters or connects.  It is highly suggested that these ground connection be made first, then folded into the rear of the box - out of the way - leaving only the short ground leads out for easy connection to devices.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 7.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Connect feed wires in the first switch box. The 2 wire cable 'feed' from the power source enters the bottom of the switch box and the hot (black) wire connects to the common or shunt terminal on the 3-way switch.  This terminal is usually identified by a different color terminal screw (often darker) from the other two terminal screws (and the green ground screw).  In the picture of the circuit above, these terminals are on the lower right side of the switches.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 8.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Connect 3 wire cable in the first switch box to the switch.  The 3 wire cable enters through the top of the first switch box. The red insulated wire connects to one of the 2 unused terminal screws (shown in the image above to be on the top left and right sides of both 3-way switches).  It does not matter to which terminal screw the wire is connected.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 9.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Connect wires in the light fixture's box.  In the light fixture's box, there will be two 3 wire cable ends hanging out. One 3 wire end comes from the first switch box that has the white insulated wire as a neutral; the other 3 wire end comes from the second switch and its white is going to be what is called the \"switch leg\".<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 10.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Connect 3 wire cable in the second switch box to the switch.  Connect the black insulated wire to the shunt or common terminal screw of the switch (once again, the common screw terminal is the different colored screw from the rest on the switch).<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 11.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Connect the fixture.  The light fixture's box should have only a black wire, a white wire and a ground wire for connection to the fixture.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 12.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Finish up.  Fold all wires carefully into the boxes and secure devices and fixture with screws.  Install plates and covers.  Restore power and test.<br><br>[[Image:Wire a 3 Way Light Switch Step 13.jpg|center|550px]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
