        {
            "id": "7431844",
            "user": "Thomscher",
            "timestamp": "20:49, 14 March 2012",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(12,508 bytes)",
            "change": -2334,
            "summary": "",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "major_deletion",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "Here is a detailed explanation of one of the hardest electrical circuits to figure out. This is one of the easiest ways to do 3-way light switch wiring.",
                    "#Select the proper wire size.  The size of all the wires in the circuit must be the same size.  If originating from an electric panel, #12 copper is the minimum size if connecting to a 20 amp circuit breaker or fuse and #14 copper is the minimum size if connecting to a 15 amp circuit breaker or fuse (it has not been permissible to use aluminum wire on circuits of these capacities for many years).  If obtaining power from a nearby electrical outlet, etc.; the new wiring must be the same size as those that supply the outlet or other circuit device.",
                    "#*NM (often called \"Romex\") and UF cable (both have plastic jacket wrapped around 2 [or more] insulated wires - one white, one black and others - and a single uninsulated wire), NM is used indoors and UF is used outdoors or buried underground.",
                    "#*BX, MC and AC types are called armored cable.  They are very similar with only slight variations (construction consists of a metal jacket formed by an interlocking, helically wound band of steel or aluminum around two [or more] insulated wires - one white, one black and others including a green or bare wire). Cables lacking a green insulated or a bare wire utilize the outer metal jacket as the ground conductor. None of these metal jacketed cables types are permitted to be installed outside or underground.",
                    "#*All of these cables have \"trade names\" derived from the number of insulated non-ground conductors they have and construction; such as: \"twelve two Romex\" or \"fourteen three BX\".  A 12/2 NM, BX, AC, or Romex cable has two #12 conductors, plus a ground.  A 14/3 NM, BX, AC or Romex cable has three #14 conductors , plus a ground.  There are special connectors for these type of cables and unique precautions and instructions for each.  NM or Romex type cable is easier to work and costs less to purchase and as such is extensively use.",
                    "#Install a 2 wire cable between the power source (outlet box, electric panel, etc.) to the first switch box.  Connect the black wire to the hot supply or circuit breaker / fuse and the white to the neutral supply to the neutral terminal bar in the panel. If connecting in an electrical panel, a the ground wire should be connected to either the neutral terminal bar or the ground terminal bar (if equipped).  If all the existing ground wires connect to one bar and all the existing white wires connect to the other, do not introduce white wires to the terminal bar with bare or green insulated wires and vice-versa.",
                    "#Connect ground wires.  All ground wires in any box or enclosure must be connected together by wire nut, lug or other approved method.  Provide as many shorter lengths (8 inch) of bare or green insulated wire as needed to this wire nut group to allow connections to green ground terminal screws on each device in the box (switches, outlets, fixtures, etc.) and if the box is metal, it too must be grounded with a green ground screw or approved clip.  No connection is made to plastic or fiber boxes.  This must be done at each and every box and device that the cable enters or connects.  It is highly suggested that these ground connection be made first, then folded into the rear of the box - out of the way - leaving only the short ground leads out for easy connection to devices.",
                    "#Connect feed wires in the first switch box. The 2 wire cable 'feed' from the power source enters the bottom of the switch box and the hot (black) wire connects to the common or shunt terminal on the 3-way switch.  This terminal is usually identified by a different color terminal screw (often darker) from the other two terminal screws (and the green ground screw).  In the picture of the circuit above, these terminals are on the lower right side of the switches."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "[[Image:Easythree-wayR.JPG|200px|thumb|3-way electric switch wiring diagram|right]]Here is a detailed explanation of one of the hardest electrical circuits to figure out. This is one of the easiest ways to do 3-way light switch wiring.",
                    "#[[Image:AWG.jpg|right|184px|thumb|The American Wire Gauge.  You can use one of these to determine wire size, but it's often easier to simply read this information printed on the cable tag or jacket.]]Select the proper wire size.  The size of all the wires in the circuit must be the same size.  If originating from an electric panel, #12 copper is the minimum size if connecting to a 20 amp circuit breaker or fuse and #14 copper is the minimum size if connecting to a 15 amp circuit breaker or fuse (it has not been permissible to use aluminum wire on circuits of these capacities for many years).  If obtaining power from a nearby electrical outlet, etc.; the new wiring must be the same size as those that supply the outlet or other circuit device.",
                    "#*[[Image:Romex_cable.agr.jpg|right|184px|thumb|2 wire NM or Romex cable.  This one is #12 gauge.]]NM (often called \"Romex\") and UF cable (both have plastic jacket wrapped around 2 [or more] insulated wires - one white, one black and others - and a single uninsulated wire), NM is used indoors and UF is used outdoors or buried underground.",
                    "#*[[Image:WTF Wiring Part 2.jpg|right|184px|thumb|Armored cable; this one appears to be the older BX type with paper wrapped conductors and makes use of the armor for ground.  The thin wire connected to the white wire by itself is not ground, but when terminated correctly assists with conductivity of ground through the armored jacket.]]BX, MC and AC types are called armored cable.  They are very similar with only slight variations (construction consists of a metal jacket formed by an interlocking, helically wound band of steel or aluminum around two [or more] insulated wires - one white, one black and others including a green or bare wire). Cables lacking a green insulated or a bare wire utilize the outer metal jacket as the ground conductor. None of these metal jacketed cables types are permitted to be installed outside or underground.",
                    "#*[[Image:Armor conn.jpg|right|184px|thumb|Armored Cable connectors.  They look similar to romex connectors, but have a reducing shoulder on the throat of the opening to hold the anti short bushing (also called a red head)..]]All of these cables have \"trade names\" derived from the number of insulated non-ground conductors they have and construction; such as: \"twelve two Romex\" or \"fourteen three BX\".  A 12/2 NM, BX, AC, or Romex cable has two #12 conductors, plus a ground.  A 14/3 NM, BX, AC or Romex cable has three #14 conductors , plus a ground.  There are special connectors for these type of cables and unique precautions and instructions for each.  NM or Romex type cable is easier to work and costs less to purchase and as such is extensively use.",
                    "#[[Image:Electrical closeup.jpg|right|177px|thumb|Panel with seperate neutral and ground bars.  The upper left corner shows a vertical silvery colored terminal strip with bare copper wires connected.  On both the left and right edges of the black area are two vertical silvery colored terminal strips that have white neutral wires connected.  Note that bare ground wires and white neutral wires are not mixed.  There is also an example of how black tape is wrapped around a white wire that is connected to a circuit breaker to indicate that it is no longer neutral.]]Install a 2 wire cable between the power source (outlet box, electric panel, etc.) to the first switch box.  Connect the black wire to the hot supply or circuit breaker / fuse and the white to the neutral supply to the neutral terminal bar in the panel. If connecting in an electrical panel, a the ground wire should be connected to either the neutral terminal bar or the ground terminal bar (if equipped).  If all the existing ground wires connect to one bar and all the existing white wires connect to the other, do not introduce white wires to the terminal bar with bare or green insulated wires and vice-versa.",
                    "#[[Image:Greenhex.jpg|right|184px|thumb|Ground Screws.  Here are a couple of #10-32 green, hex head machine screws designed to connect a ground wire to electrical boxes.  The code has maintained that the ground connection be maintained separately from screws or other fasteners used to secure or support the electrical box.]][[Image:Ground4sq.jpg|right|184px|thumb|A Ground screw installed into the factory threaded hole of a 4\" square box.]]Connect ground wires.  All ground wires in any box or enclosure must be connected together by wire nut, lug or other approved method.  Provide as many shorter lengths (8 inch) of bare or green insulated wire as needed to this wire nut group to allow connections to green ground terminal screws on each device in the box (switches, outlets, fixtures, etc.) and if the box is metal, it too must be grounded with a green ground screw or approved clip.  No connection is made to plastic or fiber boxes.  This must be done at each and every box and device that the cable enters or connects.  It is highly suggested that these ground connection be made first, then folded into the rear of the box - out of the way - leaving only the short ground leads out for easy connection to devices.",
                    "#[[Image:3way back.jpg|right|184px|thumb|The rear of a Leviton Decora Style 3-way switch.  The common or shunt terminal screw (bottom left) is black.  The other two are gold.  The remaining green screw is ground.  ]][[Image:3way shut.jpg|right|184px|thumb|A closer, side view of the Leviton 3-Way Decora Style switch.   The difference between the terminal screw connections is obvious. ]]Connect feed wires in the first switch box. The 2 wire cable 'feed' from the power source enters the bottom of the switch box and the hot (black) wire connects to the common or shunt terminal on the 3-way switch.  This terminal is usually identified by a different color terminal screw (often darker) from the other two terminal screws (and the green ground screw).  In the picture of the circuit above, these terminals are on the lower right side of the switches."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "7427418",
            "user": "Stevecon",
            "timestamp": "22:20, 13 March 2012",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(14,842 bytes)",
            "change": 72,
            "summary": "(→‎Tips)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "reverted_by": "Loni_lings",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "*[[Image:Backwire.jpg|right|184px|thumb|This is a back wireable 15 amp duplex outlet.  There are stab openings designed to accept a #14 solid copper wire.  The internal connection behind the stab opening relies on compression from a piece of spring metal against the wire that is significantly inferior to the screw terminals.  Resist using these back wireable openings.]]Use of screw terminals is preferable to the \"back wire-able\" devices that allow for simple insertion of a stripped wire into a hole for connection without the need of turning a screw.  Over time, these pressure connections fatigue and eventually fail."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "*[[Image:Backwire.jpg|right|184px|thumb|This is a back wireable duplex outlet.  There are openings designed to accept a #14 solid copper wire.  A spring metal internal connection is made to the wire that are significantly inferior to the screw terminals.  resist using these back wireable openings.]]Use of screw terminals is preferable to the \"back wire-able\" devices that allow for simple insertion of a stripped wire into a hole for connection without the need of turning a screw.  Over time, these pressure connections fatigue and eventually fail."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "7420024",
            "user": "Stevecon",
            "timestamp": "13:33, 12 March 2012",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(14,770 bytes)",
            "change": -80,
            "summary": "(→‎Steps)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "deletion",
            "reverted_by": "Loni_lings",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#[[Image:Greenhex.jpg|right|184px|thumb|Ground Screws.  Here are a couple of #10-32 green, hex head machine screws designed to connect a ground wire to electrical boxes.  The code has maintained that the ground connection be maintained separately from screws or other fasteners used to secure or support the electrical box.]][[Image:Ground4sq.jpg|right|184px|thumb|A Ground screw installed into the factory threaded hole of a 4\" square box.]]Connect ground wires.  All ground wires in any box or enclosure must be connected together by wire nut, lug or other approved method.  Provide as many shorter lengths (8 inch) of bare or green insulated wire as needed to this wire nut group to allow connections to green ground terminal screws on each device in the box (switches, outlets, fixtures, etc.) and if the box is metal, it too must be grounded with a green ground screw or approved clip.  No connection is made to plastic or fiber boxes.  This must be done at each and every box and device that the cable enters or connects.  It is highly suggested that these ground connection be made first, then folded into the rear of the box - out of the way - leaving only the short ground leads out for easy connection to devices."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#[[Image:Greenhex.jpg|right|184px|thumb|Ground Screws.  Here are a couple of #10-32 green, hex head machine screws designed to connect a ground wire to electrical boxes.  The code has maintained that the ground connection be maintained separately from screws or other fasteners used to secure or support the electrical box.]][[Image:Ground4sq.jpg|right|184px|thumb|A green, hex head #10-32 machine screw designed expressly for the connection of a ground wire; installed into the factory threaded hole of a 4\" square box.]]Connect ground wires.  All ground wires in any box or enclosure must be connected together by wire nut, lug or other approved method.  Provide as many shorter lengths (8 inch) of bare or green insulated wire as needed to this wire nut group to allow connections to green ground terminal screws on each device in the box (switches, outlets, fixtures, etc.) and if the box is metal, it too must be grounded with a green ground screw or approved clip.  No connection is made to plastic or fiber boxes.  This must be done at each and every box and device that the cable enters or connects.  It is highly suggested that these ground connection be made first, then folded into the rear of the box - out of the way - leaving only the short ground leads out for easy connection to devices."
                ]
            }
        },
