                "added": [
                    "*One terminal = one wire.  It is not permissible to connect more than one wire under a screw terminal. Additionally, wire should wrap clockwise around the screw.  Only solid wire is to be wrapped around a terminal screw.  Stranded wire will require installation of fork or ring terminals to be installed (crimped or soldered) on the wire and the terminal screw tightened down on the fork or ring terminal.",
                    "*[[Image:Backwire.jpg|right|184px|thumb|This is a back wireable duplex outlet.  There are openings designed to accept a #14 solid copper wire.  A sprig metal internal connection is made to the wire that are significantly inferior to the screw terminals.  resist using these back wireable openings.]]Use of screw terminals is preferable to the \"back wire-able\" devices that allow for simple insertion of a stripped wire into a hole for connection without the need of turning a screw.  Over time, these pressure connections fatigue and eventually fail."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "*One terminal = one wire.  It is not permissible to connect more than one wire under a screw terminal. Additionally, wire should wrap clockwise around the screw.",
                    "*Use of screw terminals is preferable to the \"back wire-able\" devices that allow for simple insertion of a stripped wire into a hole for connection without the need of turning a screw.  Over time, these pressure connections fatigue and eventually fail."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "7406133",
            "user": "Stevecon",
            "timestamp": "21:11, 9 March 2012",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(12,877 bytes)",
            "change": 611,
            "summary": "(→‎Steps:  More pictures)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "major_addition",
            "reverted_by": "Loni_lings",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#*[[Image:Armor conn.jpg|right|184px|thumb|Armored Cable connectors.  They look similar to romex connectors, but have a reducing shoulder on the throat of the opening to hold the anti short bushing (also called a red head)..]]All of these cables have \"trade names\" derived from the number of insulated non-ground conductors they have and construction; such as: \"twelve two Romex\" or \"fourteen three BX\".  A 12/2 NM, BX, AC, or Romex cable has two #12 conductors, plus a ground.  A 14/3 NM, BX, AC or Romex cable has three #14 conductors , plus a ground.  There are special connectors for these type of cables and unique precautions and instructions for each.  NM or Romex type cable is easier to work and costs less to purchase and as such is extensively use.",
                    "#[[Image:3way back.jpg|right|184px|thumb|The rear of a Leviton Decora Style 3-way switch.  The common or shunt terminal screw (bottom left) is black.  The other two are gold.  The remaining green screw is ground.  ]][[Image:3way shut.jpg|right|184px|thumb|A closer, side view of the Leviton 3-Way Decora Style switch.   The difference between the terminal screw connections is obvious. ]]Connect feed wires in the first switch box. The 2 wire cable 'feed' from the power source enters the bottom of the switch box and the hot (black) wire connects to the common or shunt terminal on the 3-way switch.  This terminal is usually identified by a different color terminal screw (often darker) from the other two terminal screws (and the green ground screw).  In the picture of the circuit above, these terminals are on the lower right side of the switches."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#*All of these cables have \"trade names\" derived from the number of insulated non-ground conductors they have and construction; such as: \"twelve two Romex\" or \"fourteen three BX\".  A 12/2 NM, BX, AC, or Romex cable has two #12 conductors, plus a ground.  A 14/3 NM, BX, AC or Romex cable has three #14 conductors , plus a ground.  There are special connectors for these type of cables and unique precautions and instructions for each.  NM or Romex type cable is easier to work and costs less to purchase and as such is extensively use.",
                    "#Connect feed wires in the first switch box. The 2 wire cable 'feed' from the power source enters the bottom of the switch box and the hot (black) wire connects to the common or shunt terminal on the 3-way switch.  This terminal is usually identified by a different color terminal screw (often darker) from the other two terminal screws (and the green ground screw).  In the picture of the circuit above, these terminals are on the lower right side of the switches."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "7405120",
            "user": "Stevecon",
            "timestamp": "14:56, 9 March 2012",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(12,266 bytes)",
            "change": 609,
            "summary": "(→‎Tips:  Added circuit design calculations.)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "major_addition",
            "reverted_by": "Loni_lings",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "*A 120V / 15A circuit is rated to support up to 1440 watts of continuous load (heat, lights, etc.), so you need a \"lot\" of lights to max-out a 15A / #14 circuit.  For comparison, a 120V / 20A circuit is rated to support up to 1920 watts of continuous load (heat, lights, etc.).  The circuit's maximum design load - in this case watts - is determined by Volts X Amps X .80, where volts and amps are given and .80 is required by the code to derate the circuit capacity to 80% of maximum.  It could be said that the maximum amperage of a 15 amp circuit is 12 amps by applying a similar formula: Circuit Breaker / Fuse X .80 = maximum amp load. The same for a 20 amp circuit: 20 X .80 = 16 amps.  The code requires all circuit to be derated to 80% of capacity.  If a larger load is to be connected, the appropriately larger sized wire and circuit breaker or fuse must be installed instead."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "*A 120V / 15A circuit is rated to support up to 1440 watts of continuous load (heat, lights, etc.), so you need a \"lot\" of lights to max-out a 15A / #14 circuit.  For comparison, a 120V / 20A circuit is rated to support up to 1920 watts of continuous load (heat, lights, etc.)."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
