        {
            "id": "7405049",
            "user": "Stevecon",
            "timestamp": "14:02, 9 March 2012",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(11,541 bytes)",
            "change": 200,
            "summary": "(→‎Steps:  AWG picture)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "reverted_by": "Loni_lings",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#[[Image:AWG.jpg|right|184px|thumb|The American Wire Gauge.  You can use one of these to determine wire size, but it's often easier to simply read this information printed on the cable tag or jacket.]]Select the proper wire size.  The size of all the wires in the circuit must be the same size.  If originating from an electric panel, #12 copper is the minimum size if connecting to a 20 amp circuit breaker or fuse and #14 copper is the minimum size if connecting to a 15 amp circuit breaker or fuse (it has not been permissible to use aluminum wire on circuits of these capacities for many years).  If obtaining power from a nearby electrical outlet, etc.; the new wiring must be the same size as those that supply the outlet or other circuit device."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Select the proper wire size.  The size of all the wires in the circuit must be the same size.  If originating from an electric panel, #12 copper is the minimum size if connecting to a 20 amp circuit breaker or fuse and #14 copper is the minimum size if connecting to a 15 amp circuit breaker or fuse (it has not been permissible to use aluminum wire on circuits of these capacities for many years).  If obtaining power from a nearby electrical outlet, etc.; the new wiring must be the same size as those that supply the outlet or other circuit device."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "7405036",
            "user": "Stevecon",
            "timestamp": "13:53, 9 March 2012",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(11,341 bytes)",
            "change": 162,
            "summary": "(→‎Steps:  Armored cable caption correction.)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "reverted_by": "Loni_lings",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#*[[Image:WTF Wiring Part 2.jpg|right|184px|thumb|Armored cable; this one appears to be the older BX type with paper wrapped conductors and makes use of the armor for ground.  The thin wire connected to the white wire by itself is not ground, but when terminated correctly assists with conductivity of ground through the armored jacket.]]BX, MC and AC types are called armored cable.  They are very similar with only slight variations (construction consists of a metal jacket formed by an interlocking, helically wound band of steel or aluminum around two [or more] insulated wires - one white, one black and others including a green or bare wire). Cables lacking a green insulated or a bare wire utilize the outer metal jacket as the ground conductor. None of these metal jacketed cables types are permitted to be installed outside or underground."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#*[[Image:WTF Wiring Part 2.jpg|right|184px|thumb|Armored cable; this one appears to be the older BX type with paper wrapped conductors and makes use of the armor for ground.]]BX, MC and AC types are called armored cable.  They are very similar with only slight variations (construction consists of a metal jacket formed by an interlocking, helically wound band of steel or aluminum around two [or more] insulated wires - one white, one black and others including a green or bare wire). Cables lacking a green insulated or a bare wire utilize the outer metal jacket as the ground conductor. None of these metal jacketed cables types are permitted to be installed outside or underground."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
