            "id": "7401758",
            "user": "Stevecon",
            "timestamp": "22:32, 8 March 2012",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(10,844 bytes)",
            "change": 106,
            "summary": "(→‎Tips:  minor edits.)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "reverted_by": "Loni_lings",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "*A 120V / 15A circuit is rated to support up to 1440 watts of continuous load (heat, lights, etc.), so you need a \"lot\" of lights to max-out a 15A / #14 circuit.",
                    "*One terminal = one wire.  It is not permissible to connect more than one wire under a screw terminal. Additionally, wire should wrap clockwise around the screw.",
                    "*Use of screw terminals is preferable to the \"back wire-able\" devices that allow for simple insertion of a stripped wire into a hole for connection without the need of turning a screw.  Over time, these pressure connections fatigue and eventually fail.",
                    "*When remodeling, always check the fuse or circuit breaker on which new lights or outlets are being installed.  If you install #14 wire connected to a circuit that is protected by a fuse or circuit breaker greater than 15 amps it's an National Electrical Code violation and, more importantly, a serious safety/fire hazard.  This actually applies to all wire.  Never install a gauge wire on a circuit that is protected by a breaker or fuse that has a higher capacity than the wire you're using: 6 gauge - 50A, 8 gauge - 40A, 10 gauge - 30A, 12 gauge - 20A, 14 gauge - 15A.  No smaller wires are permitted to be connected to the electric panel - unless they are the output of a transformer for a door bell or similar circuit."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "*A 15A circuit can support 1440 watts of lighting, so you need a \"lot\" of lights to max-out a 15A / #14 circuit.",
                    "*One terminal = one wire.  It is not permissible to connect 2 or more wires under a screw.",
                    "*Use of screw terminals is preferable to the \"back wire-able\" devices that allow for simple insertion of a stripped wire into a hole for connection without the need of turning a screw.  Over time, these pressure connections fatigue and fail.",
                    "*Remember, however, when remodeling you must always check the fuse or circuit breaker on which you're installing new lights or outlets.  If you install #14 wire connected to a circuit that is protected by a fuse or circuit breaker greater than 15 amps it's an National Electrical Code violation and, more importantly, a serious safety/fire hazard.  This actually applies to all wire.  Never install a gauge wire on a circuit that is protected by a breaker or fuse that has a higher capacity than the wire you're using: 6 gauge - 50A, 8 gauge - 40A, 10 gauge - 30A, 12 gauge - 20A, 14 gauge - 15A.  No smaller wires are permitted to be connected to the electric panel - unless they are the output of a transformer for a door bell or similar circuit."
                ]
            }
        },
