        {
            "id": "7399598",
            "user": "108.49.19.8",
            "timestamp": "13:38, 8 March 2012",
            "anon": true,
            "size": "(10,739 bytes)",
            "change": 584,
            "summary": "(→‎Steps:  Finishing up!  Now it is ready for images.)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "major_addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Select the proper wire size.  The size of all the wires in the circuit must be the same size.  If originating from an electric panel, #12 copper is the minimum size if connecting to a 20 amp circuit breaker or fuse and #14 copper is the minimum size if connecting to a 15 amp circuit breaker or fuse (it has not been permissible to use aluminum wire on circuits of these capacities for many years).  If obtaining power from a nearby electrical outlet, etc.; the new wiring must be the same size as those that supply the outlet or other circuit device.",
                    "#* NM (often called \"Romex\") and UF cable (both have plastic jacket wrapped around 2 [or more] insulated wires - one white, one black and others - and a single uninsulated wire), NM is used indoors and UF is used outdoors or buried underground.",
                    "#*BX, MC and AC cable are very similar with slight variations (construction consists of a metal jacket formed by an interlocking, helically wound band of steel or aluminum around two [or more] insulated wires - one white, one black and others including a green or bare wire). Cables lacking a green insulated or a bare wire utilize the outer metal jacket as the ground conductor. None of these metal jacketed cables types are permitted to be installed outside or underground.",
                    "#*All of these cables have \"trade names\" derived from the number of insulated non-ground conductors they have and construction; such as: \"twelve two Romex\" or \"fourteen three BX\".  A 12/2 NM, BX, AC, or Romex cable has two #12 conductors, plus a ground.  A 14/3 NM, BX, AC or Romex cable has three #14 conductors , plus a ground.  There are special connectors for these type of cables and unique precautions and instructions for each.  NM or Romex type cable is easier to work and costs less to purchase and as such is extensively use.",
                    "#Install a 2 wire cable between the power source (outlet box, electric panel, etc.) to the first switch box.  Connect the black wire to the hot supply or circuit breaker / fuse and the white to the neutral supply to the neutral terminal bar in the panel. If connecting in an electrical panel, a the ground wire should be connected to either the neutral terminal bar or the ground terminal bar (if equipped).  If all the existing ground wires connect to one bar and all the existing white wires connect to the other, do not introduce white wires to the terminal bar with bare or green insulated wires and vice-versa.",
                    "#Install a 3 wire cable from the first switch box to the light fixture's box.  A 3 wire cable has an \"extra\" wire when compared to 2 wire cables, and is nearly always coated in red insulation.  This third wire is necessary for 3-way switch installations.",
                    "#Connect ground wires.  All ground wires in any box or enclosure must be connected together by wire nut, lug or other approved method.  Provide as many shorter lengths (8 inch) of bare or green insulated wire as needed to this wire nut group to allow connections to green ground terminal screws on each device in the box (switches, outlets, fixtures, etc.) and if the box is metal, it too must be grounded with a green ground screw or approved clip.  No connection is made to plastic or fiber boxes.  This must be done at each and every box and device that the cable enters or connects.  It is highly suggested that these ground connection be made first, then folded into the rear of the box - out of the way - leaving only the short ground leads out for easy connection to devices.",
                    "#*Connect the 3 wire cable's white insulated wire (neutral) directly to the 2 wire 'feed' white insulated (neutral) wire with wire nuts.  There is no connection of any white wires to this switch.",
                    "#Connect 3 wire cable in the first switch box to the switch.  The 3 wire cable enters through the top of the first switch box. The red insulated wire connects to one of the 2 unused terminal screws (shown in the image above to be on the top left and right sides of both 3-way switches).  It does not matter to which terminal screw the wire is connected.",
                    "#Connect wires in the light fixture's box.  In the light fixture's box, there will be two 3 wire cable ends hanging out. One 3 wire end comes from the first switch box that has the white insulated wire as a neutral; the other 3 wire end comes from the second switch and its white is going to be what is called the \"switch leg\".",
                    "#*Mark both ends of the white wire in the 3 wire cable that was installed between the second switch and the light fixture box by wrapping it with black electrical tape. This alerts others working on the circuit later that the white wire in no longer neutral. This is a newer electrical code requirement but has been a common practice whenever a white or gray wire is / or can become hot.  Since this happens most often at switches, it got the name \"switch leg\"."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Select the proper wire size.  The size of all the wires in the circuit must be the same size.  If originating from an electric panel, #12 copper is the minimum size if connecting to a 20 amp circuit breaker or fuse and #14 copper is the minimum size if connecting to a 15 amp circuit breaker or fuse.  It is no longer permissible to use aluminum wire on circuits of these capacities.  If obtaining power from a nearby electrical outlet, etc.; the new wiring must be the same size as those that supply the outlet or other circuit device.",
                    "#* NM (often called \"Romex\") and UF cable (both have plastic jacket wrapped around 2 (or more) insulated wires - one white the other black - and a single uninsulated wire), NM is used indoors and UF is used outdoors or buried underground.",
                    "#*BX, MC and AC cable (construction consists of a metal jacket formed by interlocking, helically wound band of steel or aluminum around two or more insulated wires - one white, one black and others including a green or bare wire. Cables lacking a green insulated or a bare wire utilize the outer metal jacket as the ground conductor. None of these metal jacketed cables types are permitted to be installed outside or underground.  All of these cables are \"called\" by the number of insulated non-ground conductors they have and construction; such as: \"twelve two Romex\" or \"fourteen three BX\".  A 12/2 NM, BX, AC, or Romex cable has two #12 conductors, plus a ground.  A 14/3 NM, BX, AC or Romex cable has three #14 conductors , plus a ground.  There are special connectors for these type of cables and unique precautions and instructions for each.  NM or Romex type cable is easier to work and costs less to purchase and as such is extensively use.",
                    "#Install a 2 wire cable between the power source (outlet box, electric panel, etc.) to the first switch box.  Connect the black wire to the hot supply or circuit breaker / fuse and the white to the neutral supply or neutral bar in the panel.",
                    "#Install a 3 wire cable from the first switch box to the light fixture's box.  The 3 wire cables has an \"extra\" wire when compared to typical 2 wire cables, that is nearly always coated in red insulation.  This third wire is necessary for 3-way switches.",
                    "#Connect ground wires.  All ground wires in any box or enclosure must be connected together by wire nut, lug or other approved method.  Provide as many shorter lengths (8 inch) of bare or green insulated wire as needed to this wire nut group to allow connections to green ground terminal screws on each device in the box (switches, outlets, fixtures, etc.) and if the box is metal, it too must be grounded with a green ground screw or approved clip.  No connection is made to plastic or fiber boxes.  This must be done at each box and device that the cable enters or connects.  It is highly suggested that these ground connection be made first, then folded into the box - out of the way - leaving only the short ground leads out for easy connection to devices.",
                    "#*Connect the 3 wire cable's white insulated wire (neutral) directly to the 2 wire 'feed' white insulated (neutral) wire with wire nuts (there is no connection of the white wires to the switch)",
                    "#Connect 3 wire cable in the first switch box to the switch.  The 3 wire cable enters through the top of the first switch box. The red insulated wire connects to one of the 2 unused terminal screws (shown to be on both sides of the top of both 3-way switches in the picture), it does not matter which.",
                    "#Connect wires in the light fixture's box.  In the light fixture's box, there will be two 3 wire cable ends hanging out. One 3 wire end comes from the first switch box that has the white insulated wire as a neutral; the other 3 wire end comes from the second switch and its white is going to be the \"switch leg\" (more on this next).",
                    "#*Mark both ends of the white wire in the 3 wire cable that was installed between the second switch and the light fixture box by wrapping it with black electrical tape. This means you have made the white wire a 'hot' wire. This is an electrical code requirement and is a common practice any time a white or gray wire is / or can become hot.  This happens most often at switches; hence the term \"switch leg\"."
                ]
            }
        },
