        {
            "id": "7397164",
            "user": "Stevecon",
            "timestamp": "00:51, 8 March 2012",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(10,020 bytes)",
            "change": 521,
            "summary": "(→‎Tips:  a couple additions..)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "major_addition",
            "reverted_by": "Loni_lings",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "*Use the smaller, easier to work and less expensive #14 (14 gauge) copper Romex cable if the circuit is protected by a 15 amp fuse or circuit breaker.  Very few three way circuits switch loads on a 20 amp circuit.  It is not necessary to use #12 wire branching from an existing 14 wire circuit.  12 gauge is generally only used for kitchen, dining room and bath outlets and for other applications that require 20A service.",
                    "*A 15A circuit can support 1440 watts of lighting, so you need a \"lot\" of lights to max-out a 15A / #14 circuit.",
                    "*One terminal = one wire.  It is not permissible to connect 2 or more wires under a screw.",
                    "*Use of screw terminals is preferable to the \"back wire-able\" devices that allow for simple insertion of a stripped wire into a hole for connection without the need of turning a screw.  Over time, these pressure connections fatigue and fail.",
                    "*Remember, however, when remodeling you must always check the fuse or circuit breaker on which you're installing new lights or outlets.  If you install #14 wire connected to a circuit that is protected by a fuse or circuit breaker greater than 15 amps it's an National Electrical Code violation and, more importantly, a serious safety/fire hazard.  This actually applies to all wire.  Never install a gauge wire on a circuit that is protected by a breaker or fuse that has a higher capacity than the wire you're using: 6 gauge - 50A, 8 gauge - 40A, 10 gauge - 30A, 12 gauge - 20A, 14 gauge - 15A.  No smaller wires are permitted to be connected to the electric panel - unless they are the output of a transformer for a door bell or similar circuit."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "*Use the smaller, easier to work and less expensive #14 (14 gauge) copper Romex cable if the circuit is protected by a 15 amp fuse or circuit breaker.  Very few three way circuits switch loads on a 20 amp circuit.  It is not necessary to use #12 wire branching from an existing 14 wire circuit.  12 gauge is generally only used for kitchen and bath outlets and for other applications that require 20A service.",
                    "*A 15A circuit can support 1500 watts of lighting, so you need a \"lot\" of lights to max-out a 15A/#14 circuit.",
                    "*Remember, however, when remodeling you must always check the fuse or circuit breaker on which you're installing new lights or outlets.  If you install #14 wire connected to a circuit that is protected by a fuse or circuit breaker greater than 15 amps it's an National Electrical Code violation and, more importantly, a serious safety/fire hazard.  This actually applies to all wire.  Never install a gauge wire on a circuit that is protected by a breaker or fuse that has a higher capacity than the wire you're using: 6 gauge-50A, 8 gauge-40A, 10 gauge-30A, 12 gauge-20A, etc."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "7397099",
            "user": "Stevecon",
            "timestamp": "00:43, 8 March 2012",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(9,499 bytes)",
            "change": 112,
            "summary": "(→‎Steps:  a few more corrected...)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "reverted_by": "Loni_lings",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Connect feed wires in the first switch box. The 2 wire cable 'feed' from the power source enters the bottom of the switch box and the hot (black) wire connects to the common or shunt terminal on the 3-way switch.  This terminal is usually identified by a different color terminal screw (often darker) from the other two terminal screws (and the green ground screw).  In the picture of the circuit above, these terminals are on the lower right side of the switches.",
                    "#*Connect the 3 wire cable's white insulated wire (neutral) directly to the 2 wire 'feed' white insulated (neutral) wire with wire nuts (there is no connection of the white wires to the switch)",
                    "#Connect 3 wire cable in the first switch box to the switch.  The 3 wire cable enters through the top of the first switch box. The red insulated wire connects to one of the 2 unused terminal screws (shown to be on both sides of the top of both 3-way switches in the picture), it does not matter which.",
                    "#*Connect the black insulated wire to the remaining unused terminal screw on the switch.",
                    "#*Connect the red insulated wire to one of the other unused two terminal screws (it does not matter which).",
                    "#*Connect the \"switch leg\" (white wire with black tape) to the remaining unused terminal screw on the switch.",
                    "#Finish up.  Fold all wires carefully into the boxes and secure devices and fixture with screws.  Install plates and covers.  Restore power and test."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Connect feed wires in the first switch box to the switch terminals. The 2 wire cable 'feed' from the power source enters the bottom of the switch box and the hot (black) wire connects to the common or shunt terminal on the 3-way switch.  This terminal is usually identified by a different color terminal screw (often darker) from the other two terminal screws (and the green ground screw).  In the picture of the circuit above, these terminals are on the lower right side of the switches.  For clarity of discussion, we will not discuss ground terminal screw connections on switches, fixtures, etc. until the end of the article.",
                    "#Connect 3 wire cable in the first switch box to the switch.  The 3 wire cable enters through the top of the first switch box. The red insulated wire connects to one of the 2 terminal screws (shown to be on both sides of the top of both 3-way switches in the picture), it does not matter which.",
                    "#*Connect the black insulated wire to the remaining terminal screw on the 3 way switch.",
                    "#*Connect the 3 wire cable's white insulated wire (neutral) directly to the 2 wire 'feed' white insulated (neutral) wire with wire nuts (there is no connection of the white wires to the switch).",
                    "#*Connect the red insulated wire to one of the other two terminal screws (it does not matter which)."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "7397032",
            "user": "Stevecon",
            "timestamp": "00:34, 8 March 2012",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(9,387 bytes)",
            "change": -93,
            "summary": "(→‎Steps)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "deletion",
            "reverted_by": "Loni_lings",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Connect ground wires.  All ground wires in any box or enclosure must be connected together by wire nut, lug or other approved method.  Provide as many shorter lengths (8 inch) of bare or green insulated wire as needed to this wire nut group to allow connections to green ground terminal screws on each device in the box (switches, outlets, fixtures, etc.) and if the box is metal, it too must be grounded with a green ground screw or approved clip.  No connection is made to plastic or fiber boxes.  This must be done at each box and device that the cable enters or connects.  It is highly suggested that these ground connection be made first, then folded into the box - out of the way - leaving only the short ground leads out for easy connection to devices."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Connect ground wires.  All ground wires in any box or enclosure must be connected together by wire nut, lug or other acceptable method.  Provide as many shorter lengths (8 inch) as needed to this wire nut group to allow connections to green ground terminal screws on each device in the box (switches, outlets, fixtures, etc.) and if the box is metal, it too must be grounded with a green ground screw or approved clip.  No connection is made to plastic or fiber boxes.  This must be done at each box and device that the cable enters or connects.  It is highly suggested that these ground connection be made first, then folded into the box - out of the way - leaving only the short ground leads out for connection to devices.",
                    "#*Connect the \"switch leg\" (white insulated wire that was previously marked with black tape) to the remaining terminal screw."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "7397010",
            "user": "Stevecon",
            "timestamp": "00:30, 8 March 2012",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(9,480 bytes)",
            "change": 421,
            "summary": "(→‎Steps:  more clean up..)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "reverted_by": "Loni_lings",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Select the proper wire size.  The size of all the wires in the circuit must be the same size.  If originating from an electric panel, #12 copper is the minimum size if connecting to a 20 amp circuit breaker or fuse and #14 copper is the minimum size if connecting to a 15 amp circuit breaker or fuse.  It is no longer permissible to use aluminum wire on circuits of these capacities.  If obtaining power from a nearby electrical outlet, etc.; the new wiring must be the same size as those that supply the outlet or other circuit device.",
                    "#Select the proper cable type.  The power supply or feed cable should be 2 wire or conductor cable plus a ground wire.  Common cable types with these features are:",
                    "#* NM (often called \"Romex\") and UF cable (both have plastic jacket wrapped around 2 (or more) insulated wires - one white the other black - and a single uninsulated wire), NM is used indoors and UF is used outdoors or buried underground.",
                    "#*BX, MC and AC cable (construction consists of a metal jacket formed by interlocking, helically wound band of steel or aluminum around two or more insulated wires - one white, one black and others including a green or bare wire. Cables lacking a green insulated or a bare wire utilize the outer metal jacket as the ground conductor. None of these metal jacketed cables types are permitted to be installed outside or underground.  All of these cables are \"called\" by the number of insulated non-ground conductors they have, such as \"12/3 Romex\" or \"14/2 BX\".  A 12/2 NM, BX, AC, or Romex cable has two #12 conductors, plus a ground.  A 14/3 NM, BX, AC or Romex cable has three #12 conductors , plus a ground.  There are special connectors for these type of cables and unique precautions and instructions for each.",
                    "#Install a 3 wire cable from the first switch box to the light fixture's box.  The 3 wire cables has an \"extra\" wire when compared to typical 2 wire cables, that is nearly always coated in red insulation.  This third wire is necessary for 3-way switches.",
                    "#Install a 3 wire cable from the second switch box to the light fixture's box.",
                    "#Connect ground wires.  All ground wires in any box or enclosure must be connected together by wire nut, lug or other acceptable method.  Provide as many shorter lengths (8 inch) as needed to this wire nut group to allow connections to green ground terminal screws on each device in the box (switches, outlets, fixtures, etc.) and if the box is metal, it too must be grounded with a green ground screw or approved clip.  No connection is made to plastic or fiber boxes.  This must be done at each box and device that the cable enters or connects.  It is highly suggested that these ground connection be made first, then folded into the box - out of the way - leaving only the short ground leads out for connection to devices.",
                    "#*Connect the \"switch leg\" (white insulated wire that was previously marked with black tape) to the remaining terminal screw.",
                    "#Connect feed wires in the first switch box to the switch terminals. The 2 wire cable 'feed' from the power source enters the bottom of the switch box and the hot (black) wire connects to the common or shunt terminal on the 3-way switch.  This terminal is usually identified by a different color terminal screw (often darker) from the other two terminal screws (and the green ground screw).  In the picture of the circuit above, these terminals are on the lower right side of the switches.  For clarity of discussion, we will not discuss ground terminal screw connections on switches, fixtures, etc. until the end of the article.",
                    "#*Connect the black insulated wire to the remaining terminal screw on the 3 way switch.",
                    "#*Connect the 3 wire cable's white insulated wire (neutral) directly to the 2 wire 'feed' white insulated (neutral) wire with wire nuts (there is no connection of the white wires to the switch).",
                    "#*Mark both ends of the white wire in the 3 wire cable that was installed between the second switch and the light fixture box by wrapping it with black electrical tape. This means you have made the white wire a 'hot' wire. This is an electrical code requirement and is a common practice any time a white or gray wire is / or can become hot.  This happens most often at switches; hence the term \"switch leg\".",
                    "#Connect 3 wire cable in the second switch box to the switch.  Connect the black insulated wire to the shunt or common terminal screw of the switch (once again, the common screw terminal is the different colored screw from the rest on the switch)."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Select the proper wire size.  The size of all the wires in the circuit must be the same size.  If originating from an electric panel, #12 copper is the minimum size if connecting to a 20 amp circuit breaker or fuse and #14 copper is the minimum size if connecting to a 15 amp circuit breaker or fuse.  It is no longer permissible to use aluminum wire on circuits of these capacities.  If obtaining power from a nearby electrical outlet, etc.; the new wiring must be the same size as those that supply the outlet or light fixture.",
                    "#Select the proper cable type.  The power supply or feed cable should be 2 wire or conductor cable plus a ground wire.  Common cable types with these features are NM and UF cables often called \"Romex\" (a plastic jacket wrapped around 2 insulated wires -one white the other black - wires and a single uninsulated wire), NM is used indoors and UF is used outdoors or buried underground.  BX, MC and AC types (construction consists of a metal jacket formed by interlocking, helically wound band of steel or aluminum around two or three insulated - one white, one black and the third green - wires) cables. Cables consisting of just two insulated wires utilize the outer metal jacket as the third conductor or ground. None of these metal jacketed cables types are permitted outside or underground.  All of these cables are \"called\" by the number of insulated non-ground conductors they have, such as \"12/3 Romex\".  A 12/2 NM, BX, AC, or Romex cable has two #12 conductors, plus a ground.  A 14/3 NM, BX, AC or Romex cable has three #12 conductors , plus a ground.",
                    "#Install a 3 wire cable from the first switch box to the light fixture's box.  The 3 wire cables has an extra wire when compared to typical 2 wire cables, that is nearly always wrapped in red insulation.  This third wire is necessary for 3-way switches.",
                    "#Install a 3 wire cable from the second switch box to the light fixture's box.",
                    "#Connect feed wires in the first switch box to the switch. At the first switch box, the 2 wire cable 'feed' from the power source enters the bottom of the switch box and the hot (black) wire connects to the common or shunt terminal on the 3-way switch.  This terminal is usually identified by a different color terminal screw (often darker) from the other two terminal screws and the green ground screw.  In the picture of the circuit above, these terminals are on the lower right side of the switches.  For clarity of discussion, we will not discuss ground terminal screw connections on switches, fixtures, etc. until the end of the article.",
                    "#*The black insulated wire connects to the remaining terminal screw on the 3-way switch.",
                    "#*The 3 wire cable's white insulated wire (neutral) connects directly to the 2 wire 'feed' white insulated (neutral) wire with wire nuts (there is no connection of the white wires to the switch).",
                    "#*Mark each end of the white wire in the 3 wire cable that was installed between the second switch and the light fixture box by wrapping it with black electrical tape. This means you have made the white wire a 'hot' wire. This is an electrical code requirement and is a common practice any time a white or gray wire is / or can become hot.  This happens most often at switches, hence the term \"switch leg\".",
                    "#Connect 3 wire cable in the second switch box to the switch.  Connect the black insulated wire to the shunt or common terminal screw of the switch (the common screw terminal is the different colored screw from the rest on the switch).",
                    "#*Connect the \"switch leg\" (white insulated wire that was previously marked with black tape) to the remaining terminal screw",
                    "#Connect ground wires.  All ground wires in any box or enclosure must be connected together by wire nut, lug or other acceptable method.  Include as many as needed shorter lengths (8 inch) added to this wire nut group to provide connections to green ground terminal screw on each device in the box (switches, outlets, fixtures, etc.) and the box too, if of metal construction with a green ground screw or approved clip.  No connection is made to plastic or fiber boxes."
                ]
            }
        },
