        {
            "id": "7396263",
            "user": "Stevecon",
            "timestamp": "22:20, 7 March 2012",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(9,056 bytes)",
            "change": 2904,
            "summary": "(→‎Steps:  Rewrite - except for AUS methods.  Needs photos.)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "major_addition",
            "reverted_by": "Loni_lings",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Select the proper wire size.  The size of all the wires in the circuit must be the same size.  If originating from an electric panel, #12 copper is the minimum size if connecting to a 20 amp circuit breaker or fuse and #14 copper is the minimum size if connecting to a 15 amp circuit breaker of fuse.  It is no longer permissible to use aluminum wire on circuits of these capacities.  If obtaining power from a nearby electrical outlet, etc.; the new wiring must be the same size as those that supply the outlet or light fixture.",
                    "#Select the proper cable type.  The power supply or feed cable should be 2 wire or conductor cable plus a ground wire.  Common cable types with these features are NM and UF cables often called \"Romex\" (a plastic jacket wrapped around 2 insulated wires -one white the other black - wires and a single uninsulated wire), NM is used indoors and UF is used outdoors or buried underground.  BX, MC and AC types (construction consists of a metal jacket formed by interlocking, helically wound band of steel or aluminum around two or three insulated - one white, one black and the third green - wires) cables. Cables consisting of just two insulated wires utilize the outer metal jacket as the third conductor or ground. None of these metal jacketed cables types are permitted outside or underground.  All of these cables are \"called\" by the number of insulated non-ground conductors they have, such as \"12/3 Romex\".  A 12/2 NM, BX, AC, or Romex cable has two #12 conductors, plus a ground.  A 14/3 NM, BX, AC or Romex cable has three #12 conductors , plus a ground.",
                    "#Shut power off.  This is a very important step.  Please do not skip it.",
                    "#Install a 2 wire cable between the power source (outlet box, electric panel, etc.) to the first switch box.  Connect the black wire to the hot supply or circuit breaker / fuse and the white to the neutral supply or neutral bar in the panel.",
                    "#Install a 3 wire cable from the first switch box to the light fixture's box.  The 3 wire cables has an extra wire when compared to typical 2 wire cables, that is nearly always wrapped in red insulation.  This third wire is necessary for 3-way switches.",
                    "#Install a 3 wire cable from the second switch box to the light fixture's box.",
                    "#Connect feed wires in the first switch box to the switch. At the first switch box, the 2 wire cable 'feed' from the power source enters the bottom of the switch box and the hot (black) wire connects to the common or shunt terminal on the 3-way switch.  This terminal is usually identified by a different color terminal screw (often darker) from the other two terminal screws and the green ground screw.  In the picture of the circuit above, these terminals are on the lower right side of the switches.  For clarity of discussion, we will not discuss ground terminal screw connections on switches, fixtures, etc. until the end of the article.",
                    "#Connect 3 wire cable the first switch box to the switch.  The 3 wire cable enters through the top of the first switch box. The red insulated wire connects to one of the 2 terminal screws (shown to be on both sides of the top of both 3-way switches in the picture), it does not matter which.",
                    "#*The black insulated wire connects to the remaining terminal screw on the 3-way switch.",
                    "#*The 3 wire cable's white insulated wire (neutral) connects directly to the 2 wire 'feed' white insulated (neutral) wire with wire nuts (there is no connection of the white wires to the switch).",
                    "#Connect wires in the light fixture's box.  In the light fixture's box, there will be two 3 wire cable ends hanging out. One 3 wire end comes from the first switch box that has the white insulated wire as a neutral; the other 3 wire end comes from the second switch and its white is going to be the \"switch leg\" (more on this next).",
                    "#*Mark each end of the white wire in the 3 wire cable that was installed between the second switch and the light fixture box by wrapping it with black electrical tape. This means you have made the white wire a 'hot' wire. This is an electrical code requirement and is a common practice any time a white or gray wire is / or can become hot.  This happens most often at switches, hence the term \"switch leg\".",
                    "#*Connect the two red insulated wires together with a wire nut.",
                    "#*Connect the black wire coming from the first 3-way switch and the white \"switch leg\" wire coming from the second 3-way switch (has black tape wrapped around it) together with a wire nut.",
                    "#Connect 3 wire cable in the second switch box to the switch.  Connect the black insulated wire to the shunt or common terminal screw of the switch (the common screw terminal is the different colored screw from the rest on the switch).",
                    "#*Connect the red insulated wire to one of the other two terminal screws (it does not matter which).",
                    "#*Connect the \"switch leg\" (white insulated wire that was previously marked with black tape) to the remaining terminal screw",
                    "#Connect ground wires.  All ground wires in any box or enclosure must be connected together by wire nut, lug or other acceptable method.  Include as many as needed shorter lengths (8 inch) added to this wire nut group to provide connections to green ground terminal screw on each device in the box (switches, outlets, fixtures, etc.) and the box too, if of metal construction with a green ground screw or approved clip.  No connection is made to plastic or fiber boxes.",
                    "#The light box should now have only a black wire, a white wire and a ground wire for connection to the fixture."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#The 'feed' cable should be 12-2 Romex (12 gauge for 20A Circuit, 14 gauge for 15A Circuit) right up to the first switch, then run 12-3 Romex 'traveler cable' to the light and then on to the second switch.",
                    "#The 12-3 Romex has an extra wire called a traveler (usually red), which is necessary for 3-way switches.",
                    "#At the first 3-way switch, the 12-2 Romex 'feed' from the circuit enters the bottom of the switch box and the hot (black) wire connects to the bottom right terminal on the 3-way switch.  This terminal is referred to as the common terminal and is usually identified with a different color screw from the other two and the ground screw.",
                    "#The 12-3 Romex 'traveler cable' enters through the top of the switch box. The red traveler (hot) wire connects to top left terminal on the 3-way switch. The black (hot) wire connects to the top right terminal on the 3-way switch.  The red traveler and the black wire are electrically indistinguishable and are actually the two poles that are switched when you flip one of the 3-way switches up or down.",
                    "#The 12-3 neutral (white) wire connects directly to the 12-2 'feed' neutral wire with wire nuts and the copper ground wires are also connected together with wire nuts.",
                    "#At the light box, you will eventually have two ends of 12-3 Romex hanging out. One end comes from the first switch (neutral); the other end comes from the second switch (hot switch leg).",
                    "#At the light box, connect the copper ground wires together with a wire nut to get them out of the way, and then connect the two red traveler wires together with a wire nut as well.",
                    "#At the light box, find the black wire coming from the first 3-way switch and the white wire coming from the second 3-way switch and connect them together with wire nuts. Now, and this is important, you need to mark each end of this white wire by wrapping it with black electrical tape. This means you have made the white wire a 'hot' wire. This is a code requirement and is a common practice for 3 and 4-way switches.",
                    "#Moving on to the second 3-way switch, you will connect the red traveler wire to the top left terminal. The white wire, now labeled as black with tape, connects to the upper right terminal. The black wire, as usual, will connect to the lower right common terminal screw. As in the first switch box and light box, the copper ground wire will connect to the box if it's a metal box or the green ground screw on the switch/fixture if the box is plastic.",
                    "#The light box should now have a black wire, a white wire and perhaps a ground wire (for a plastic box) sticking out for installing your fixture."
                ]
            }
        },
